// builtinDocs maps each built-in check name to its package doc comment
// and the config keys it reads, as Markdown.
var builtinDocs = map[string]string{
	"adr-checks":                   "Keeps each architecture decision record in the README of the check that proves it. An ADR is prose, so a decision holds only while a check in `fitness/adr-NNNN/` proves it, and that folder's README.md is the ADR. A requirement doc holds the same way, while its tests pass.\n\nThe check applies to a repo with a `fitness/` folder of its own checks. A repo without one passes with `filesChecked: 0`. The [local checks guide](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/docs/local-checks.md) shows how to write a check.\n\n## The ADR\n\nA `fitness/adr-NNNN/README.md` opens with the ADR's title and a one-line summary, which `fitness list` shows. Its `## Context`, `## Decision` and `## Consequences` sections state the decision. A `## Check` section after them says what the check fails and how to pass it.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Home: a numbered `NNNN-kebab-title.md` ADR in a folder named `adr`, outside `fitness/`, fails. Move it into its check's README.\n- Shape: an `adr-NNNN` README that lacks a decision section fails.\n- Check: each ADR folder needs a check program, unless superseded; the run fails before any check starts without one.\n- Changes: an ADR whose decision sections changed fails unless another file of its check changed too.\n- Rewrites: the check's code, or its README's own part, may change alone, since the decision still holds.\n- Moves: a README that held no decision at the base is new, so moving an ADR into it needs no code change.\n- Scope: a full scan judges no changes; a commit judges its staged files, and a pull request its diff.\n\nA paired check that judges no files fails as a broken scan. So an ADR check whose file list goes empty never passes by reading nothing.\n",
	"architecture-actors":          "Requires each requirement's Given to start with an actor the system context declares. No role slips into a requirement that the architecture does not know, and a dropped role fails every Given that still names it.\n\nThe check reads the model that [architecture-contract](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-architecture-contract/README.md) reads and the Given text that [requirements](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-requirements/README.md) parses. It adds no fields, columns, or rows.\n\n## Actors\n\nAn actor is a person in `01-system-context.md`: a C4 `Person` or `Person_Ext`. Its name is the label without the callout number, and its Mermaid ID is the one alias the diagram declares.\n\n- `Person(developer, \"1 Developer\")` declares the name `Developer` and the alias `developer`.\n- `Person_Ext(host, \"2 Host\")` declares the name `Host` and the alias `host`.\n\nThe system context is a `C4Context` diagram, as its [template](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/architecture/templates/01-system-context.md) draws it. A flowchart node classed `person` is no actor, so a Given that names one fails until the view becomes C4. Systems, legends, and people in other views are never actors.\n\n## Grammar\n\nA Given starts with one actor, or several joined by `and`, then the state. A spelling matches only as a whole word, with the diagram's case.\n\n- `Given Host has no network` passes.\n- `Given host has no network` passes, by ID.\n- `Given Host and Participant have joined` passes.\n- `Given Hosts have joined` fails: no whole-word actor.\n- `Given Admin has joined` fails: undeclared.\n- `Given I have joined` fails unless `I` is an actor's name or ID.\n- `Given no network` fails: no actor.\n- `Given Host and` fails: no actor after `and`.\n\nNot every actor needs a Given; the check constrains the roles requirements use.\n\n## Failures\n\n- No actor at the start, or after `and`: the Given line, the context diagram line, and the declared names.\n- A spelling that is one actor's ID and another actor's name: the Given line and both context lines.\n\nEvery run reads every requirement doc and the whole model, scoped runs included, so a removed actor fails even when no requirement changed.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- No architecture views: pass with `filesChecked: 0`.\n- Otherwise `filesChecked` counts the requirement docs read.\n- Doc shape and missing views belong to the `requirements` and `architecture-contract` checks.\n\n## Requirements\n\n[Requirement 0074](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/docs/requirements/0074-checks-architecture-actors.md) has one installed-command test per acceptance.\n",
	"architecture-code":            "Resolves each class of the code view to a declaration a language module's reader reads. It keeps the relations between them to the edges the view draws. It reads the model `architecture-contract` describes and adds no fields or columns. Labels, class bodies, arrows, annotations, and row links already say it all.\n\n## Classes\n\nA class is concrete unless it carries `<<abstract>>`. A concrete class resolves by four facts the view already states.\n\n- File: a link to a source a view reader reads, in its callout row; a `#L3-L9` anchor narrows it to those lines.\n- Full name: the label, such as `checkkit.Result`, `Store.save`, or `resolveChecks`; a qualifier is a package or an owning type.\n- Kind: parentheses mean a function or method; none mean a type. `<<interface>>` requires an interface declaration.\n- Signature: the parameter types in parentheses, each as `type`, `name type`, `type name`, or `name: type`, then an optional result.\n\n`()` states no parameters, and a result may be a list such as `(Record, error)`. A method may sit in another file than its type. Class body lines are members: `+Name`, `+Name: Type`, or `+Type Name` is a field; `+Run(string) error` is a method. A type's extensions add members too, in a language that has them.\n\nMermaid generics such as `Promise~boolean~` read as `Promise<boolean>`, and `List~? extends T~` as `List<? extends T>`. A field's type is every word before its name. Spaces beside brackets and colons never matter.\n\n## Class failures\n\n- A class whose linked lines lack the symbol fails, naming where the symbol is declared now, or that it is declared nowhere.\n- A kind, signature, member, or field type that differs fails, naming the class line and the source line.\n- One name declared twice, under two build conditions or as overloads, fails listing both, until a line link or signature picks one.\n- A class linking a test or generated source fails. A link to any other file type is unsupported evidence.\n- Two classes naming one declaration fail, naming both.\n- A label that is not a symbol, such as `Run environment`, fails until the class is marked `<<abstract>>`.\n\n## Readers\n\nEach [language module](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/langs/langs.go) brings one view reader through the shared code model. The reader's package, `go/internal/lang/<language>/views`, documents what it reads, which types the view covers, and its limits.\n\n## Coverage\n\nThe view covers top-level exported types, as each reader marks them. Every such type in a product file of a folder the view already shows needs a class. Functions, fields, and local bindings may be left out.\n\n## Relations\n\nRelations between two concrete classes come from the source, never from text that merely matches. Each reader observes them in its own files: calls, references, embedding, inheritance, and conformance.\n\nA use belongs to the smallest class whose lines hold it: a method's own class, else the type that owns it. A reader may follow a call into a function the view does not show, so a diagram may leave helpers out.\n\n- `-->`, `..>`, `*--`, `o--` allow a call, reference, or embedding from the first class to the second.\n- `--`, `..` allow the same, in either direction.\n- `<|--` allows embedding, or inheritance from a superclass or interface.\n- `..|>` allows conformance to an interface the type states, or one its reader decides it satisfies.\n\n## Relation failures\n\n- A relation no drawn edge allows fails at its source line, naming the view.\n- A drawn edge between two concrete classes is required: no source relation of its kind fails at the edge.\n- An edge row that links a source must hold such a relation within its lines.\n- A realization a reader decides needs a link that holds the type or one of its methods.\n- An edge touching an abstract class is allowed and needs no use. A link on it is unsupported evidence.\n- Any other arrow is unsupported.\n\n## Scope\n\nEvery run reads the whole model and every source, so a change to only the code or only the view still fails.\n\n## Requirements\n\n[Requirement 0076](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/docs/requirements/0076-checks-architecture-code.md) has one installed-command test per acceptance.\n",
	"architecture-components":      "Gives every product source one owner among the components the components view draws. It keeps the references between components, and the runtimes that reach them, to what the view draws. It reads the model `architecture-contract` describes and adds no fields or columns.\n\n## Owners\n\nA component owns the product sources its claims cover: a path in its diagram strings or its callout row. Boundaries, people, and external systems own nothing; their claims only scope the components inside them.\n\nProduct sources are the product category of the `architecture-contract` inventory, so tests, tools, and generated files need no owner. These sources are tools:\n\n- A root-level file a language module names a tool config; the same file deeper down is product code.\n- A source under the top-level `scripts/` folder.\n- A file only a manifest's scripts run, as the language module that reads the manifest finds it.\n- Neither of the last two when the product starts it or publishes it.\n\nOwnership fails or holds as follows:\n\n- A product source no component names fails, naming the source.\n- Agent tooling folders, such as `.claude/` and `.codex/`, get no exemption: their sources need an owner, and their references a drawn edge.\n- A code span or label claims a root-level source by its bare name; any other bare name stays prose.\n- A source two components name, in two diagrams of one view, fails naming both claims. Two claims in one diagram fail in `architecture-contract`.\n- A claimed path that no longer exists fails in `architecture-contract`.\n\n## References\n\nEvery reference between two owners is an observed edge. The view must draw it, from the component or a boundary enclosing it to the other component or a boundary enclosing that one. An edge the view does not draw fails at the reference, naming the view and any drawn edge in the other direction. A drawn edge no reference uses is allowed.\n\nEach [language module](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/langs/langs.go) whose view reader lists references finds them in its product sources. A reference is an import of another folder, or a name that resolves to a declaration in another file. The reader's package, `go/internal/lang/<language>/views`, documents how it resolves them.\n\nComments and string literals create no reference. A source its reader cannot parse fails. A reference under a build condition keeps it, and the error names it after `under`. Generated sources stay in the inventory but are not read.\n\n## Evidence\n\nAn edge row that links a source file requires the edge; a row without a link allows it. The link must be a source whose reader lists references, owned by the edge's first component. Its lines, or the whole file without an anchor, must name the second component: import its folder, or name a symbol it declares. Anything else fails, naming the row and the link.\n\nFor an external system the lines must name it outside comments, by a word of its name, technology, or description.\n\n## Runtimes\n\nThe runtimes are the products `architecture-containers` matches to containers. A component under a boundary whose ID is a container of the containers view belongs to that runtime. Each runtime is walked from the components its product folders are built from, along the observed edges.\n\nReaching a component declared under another runtime's container fails at the reference, naming that boundary. A component outside every container boundary is shared and may be reached by any runtime.\n\n## Scope\n\nEvery run reads the whole model and every product source, so a change to only the code or only the view still fails.\n\n## Requirements\n\n[Requirement 0075](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/docs/requirements/0075-checks-architecture-components.md) has one installed-command test per acceptance.\n",
	"architecture-containers":      "Matches the containers view against the runtime products the repository builds and deploys. A new server, a missing native target, a wrong technology, or a source layer drawn as a runtime fails. The view comes from the model `architecture-contract` reads; this check adds no fields or columns.\n\n## What ships\n\nEach product is proved by a build target, an entry point, or a deploy file. A dependency manifest alone proves nothing, so a native app may keep another language's packages for lint and build tools. Build manifests are read even when `.gitattributes` marks them `linguist-generated`. Files under test or tool folders are source types, never products.\n\nEach [language module](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/langs/langs.go) brings a runtime reader. Its `Runtime` documentation names the evidence it reads and the products it proves. It also names their container's technology and the platform services its sources use.\n\n## What the view says\n\nAn owned container is a `Container`, `ContainerDb`, or `ContainerQueue` inside a boundary. A container owns a product when a path it names covers the product's folder, or when its name is the product's name. A trailing `*` in a path, such as `go/cmd/fitness-check-*`, names every sibling folder it matches. A path of `.` names the repo root, so it covers a product built from the root folder and no other.\n\nA container also owns a package a code span or label names, or a path covering an entry point its reader lists. A description word counts only as the exact entry point, and trailing punctuation never counts.\n\nExternal systems, `System_Ext` and `Container_Ext`, need no evidence. A platform service the code uses needs an external system whose name, technology, or description names it.\n\n## Failures\n\nEvery error names the view line or the evidence line; an error about both names both.\n\n- A product no owned container claims, naming its entry point, target, or deploy file.\n- A product two containers claim.\n- A `Container` that matches no product: by name, or through paths that hold only source layers.\n- A container whose technology does not name its product's language.\n- An owned store that names no schema, configuration, or source path.\n- A platform service the code uses with no external system naming it.\n- A target without a type, a target source folder that does not exist, or a manifest the check cannot parse.\n\n## Runs\n\nNo architecture views means a pass with zero files; `filesChecked` counts the container views. Scoped runs read every view and every product, so a change on either side still meets the other.\n\n## Requirements\n\n[Requirement 0073](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/docs/requirements/0073-checks-architecture-containers.md) has one installed-command test per acceptance.\n",
	"architecture-contract":        "Validates declarations beside all five architecture views before more specific checks resolve their proof. It reads one model from the diagrams and callout tables already in each view, and adds no fields or columns. Later checks share the model through `internal/architecture.Load` and requirement clauses through `internal/requirement.Load`.\n\n## Views\n\nThe roots are `architecture/` and `docs/architecture/`. Both roots add to one model.\n\n- `01-system-context.md`: people and systems.\n- `02-containers.md`: runtime units.\n- `03-components.md`: components and their sources.\n- `04-code.md`: classes and functions.\n- `05-user-journey.md`: journey steps and the requirement IDs they fulfil.\n\nNo views means a pass with zero files, unless the repo has product code in a [language module](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/langs/langs.go)'s sources. Then the check fails and asks for all five. Product code is any such tracked file except tests, tool configs, and files marked `linguist-generated`. Each module names its own tests and tool configs.\n\nAny view requires all five, and each view needs a diagram outside its legends. Scoped runs read the whole model.\n\n## Templates\n\nEach view has a template in [internal/architecture/templates](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/architecture/templates), and fitness ships all five inside the binary. Start each view the repo lacks as a copy of its template:\n\n```\nmkdir -p docs/architecture\nfor view in 01-system-context 02-containers 03-components 04-code 05-user-journey; do\n  test -e \"docs/architecture/$view.md\" || curl -fsSL -o \"docs/architecture/$view.md\" \\\n    \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/v1/go/internal/architecture/templates/$view.md\"\ndone\n```\n\nThe `doc-template` check judges every view under `docs/architecture/` or `architecture/` against its shipped template. A repo needs no copy of the templates; see [shipped templates](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-doc-template/README.md#shipped-templates). Each template's comments say what to write. Each level has its own diagram, kinds, and palette:\n\n- 01 System context: `C4Context`, with Person, System, and `System_Ext`; System `#1d4ed8`.\n- 02 Containers: `C4Container`, with Person, `System_Boundary`, Container, and `System_Ext`; Container `#0f766e`.\n- 03 Components: `C4Component`, with Person, `Container_Boundary`, Component, and `System_Ext`; Component `#b45309`.\n- 04 Code: `classDiagram`, with classes; class `#6d28d9`.\n- 05 User journey: `flowchart TB`, with the four journey classes and its `classDef` lines.\n\nPeople are slate `#334155` and outside systems grey `#64748b` on every level, all with white text. Each fill is a fixed color that keeps 4.5:1 contrast with white, so GitHub's light and dark themes draw it alike.\n\n## What the diagrams already say\n\nEach element a diagram declares carries these facts:\n\n- Identity: the Mermaid ID, such as `user` in `Person(user, \"1 User\")`; the same ID in two views is one element.\n- Kind: the C4 macro, flowchart `:::class`, or `class`.\n- Name: the label without its callout number.\n- Parent: the enclosing C4 boundary, subgraph, or namespace.\n- Callout number: a display reference that binds a table row; renumbering keeps identity.\n\nIts relations and class bodies carry these:\n\n- A directed relationship: `Rel(...)` or an arrow; left-facing class arrows and `Rel_Back` are reversed.\n- A relationship kind, kept for the code check: the arrow itself, such as `-->` or `..>`.\n- A member of the class: a class body line, such as `+Ok` or `+Run(string) error`.\n- An annotation of the class: a class annotation, such as `<<abstract>>`.\n\nLegends stay out of the model: unnumbered diagrams, C4 diagrams titled `Legend`, and diagrams under a `Legend` heading with their tables.\n\n## What callout rows already say\n\n- A code span or diagram text naming a path under a tracked top-level folder claims that source for the element.\n- A code span or label naming a tracked root-level source, such as `index.ts`, claims that source.\n- A trailing `*` in such a path, as in `go/cmd/fitness-check-*`, claims every sibling the pattern matches.\n- A link to a tracked source file, optionally `#L3` or `#L3-L9`, is static evidence for the element or edge.\n- A link to `docs/requirements/NNNN-*.md`, or a code span such as `0001.1`, is a requirement reference.\n\nOther text is prose. For example, a consumer's `node_modules/.bin` is no claim, since `node_modules` is not a tracked folder.\n\n## Failures\n\n- Duplicate or invalid IDs, and edges to IDs the diagram does not declare.\n- A container in a container view, or a component in a component view, outside any boundary.\n- One person ID with two names, or one person name on two IDs.\n- Two elements claiming one source path when neither encloses the other.\n- Claimed paths, evidence files, or line ranges that do not exist.\n- Requirement references that resolve to no doc or acceptance. Doc shape errors belong to the `requirements` check.\n- Unsupported Mermaid forms and statements.\n\nFront matter, accessibility lines, C4 `Update…` style macros, and named C4 arguments such as `$tags` are styling and stay out of the model.\n\nEvery error names the view line. An error about two places names both.\n\n## Sources and proof\n\nEvery tracked source stays in the inventory as product, test, tool, or generated, with the elements whose claims cover it. Tool sources are scripts and root-level script tool configs such as `rollup.config.mjs`. Unowned sources are listed, not failed; the component check decides ownership.\n\nStatic evidence only locates a line; later checks resolve what it proves. Runtime proof has no form in the diagrams, so no edge counts as proved at runtime.\n\n## Requirements\n\n[Requirement 0070](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/docs/requirements/0070-checks-architecture-contract.md) has one installed-command test per acceptance.\n",
	"architecture-journey":         "Keeps the user journey diagram and the requirement docs covering each other. Every requirement doc ID appears in a journey diagram label, and every journey node that promises behavior names one. It reads the shared model from `internal/architecture.Load`. Scoped runs still read the whole journey view and every requirement doc.\n\n## Requirement IDs in the diagram\n\nThe journey view (`05-user-journey.md` under `architecture/` or `docs/architecture/`) names each requirement doc in its Mermaid diagram, on the step that fulfils it:\n\n```\nflowchart TB\n    user((1 User)):::persona\n    open(2 Open the app<br/>Req 0001, 0002):::action\n    subgraph saving[\"Saving<br/>Req 0003\"]\n        done[3 Record saved]:::screen\n    end\n    user --> open\n    open -->|saves 0004| done\n```\n\nLabels follow these rules:\n\n- Any four-digit number in a node, subgraph, or edge label is a requirement ID. Write it after the callout number, never first.\n- A Mermaid character code such as `#9829;` is not an ID.\n- An acceptance such as `0001.1` is not a doc ID. Acceptances keep their one owning test under the `requirements` check.\n- One label names at most eight IDs. A step that fulfils more splits into steps, such as one node per check family.\n- One ID may sit on several steps. Tables and prose outside the diagram cover nothing.\n\nPersonas and layout subgraphs promise no behavior and need no ID. Legends stay out of the model. Every other node, such as a screen, action, or system step, names an ID in its own label or an enclosing subgraph label. An edge label covers its IDs but no node.\n\n## Failures\n\nEvery error names the view line it concerns and the requirement ID.\n\n- A requirement doc no label names, at the diagram's fence line.\n- A label naming an acceptance ID or a doc that does not exist.\n- A label naming more than eight IDs.\n- A behavior node with no ID, at its diagram line.\n\nA repo without architecture views passes with zero files. A missing journey view belongs to `architecture-contract`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n[Requirement 0072](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/docs/requirements/0072-checks-architecture-journey.md) has one installed-command test per acceptance.\n",
	"build-output-untracked":       "Keeps build output out of git and out of source imports. It is on by default.\n\n## Build output is not tracked\n\nRule A covers the `dist` directory and the output each language module names:\n\n- `dist` must be git-ignored, as `git check-ignore -q dist` verifies. Fail hint: add `dist/` to `.gitignore`.\n- `dist` must have no tracked files: `git ls-files -- dist` and `git ls-files -- '**/dist/**'` are both empty.\n- The fail hint for tracked files: remove the listed files with `git rm --cached`.\n- The build output each [language module](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/langs/langs.go) finds must have no tracked files either. They join the same list, in one combined error.\n\n## No imports from build output\n\nRule B scans every tracked `.ts`, `.mts`, and `.cts` file, test and spec files included. It flags any `from '…'`, `import('…')`, or `require('…')` whose specifier reaches into a `dist/` path, such as `../dist/x.js`, and reports it as `path:line`.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Git commands run from the repo root; only their stdout is parsed, so a failing git command contributes no phantom tracked files.\n- `filesChecked` counts the scanned source files plus one for the dist git checks.\n- Each module's package docs name its output paths. A repo with no such output and no `dist` rules to judge passes clean.\n",
	"changelog":                    "Judges the `changelog/` entry files, the only changelog source. Each must hold one dated `### yyyy.mm.dd.HHMM` heading and its bullets, and its name must start with that heading's date. `CHANGELOG.md` is not judged, so a repo may keep, freeze, or drop it, and an upstream fork's `### Added` history passes as it is. The package version is not judged either.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: every entry file holds one dated heading and carries its date in its name.\n- Pass with zero files: a repo with no entry files, with or without `CHANGELOG.md`.\n- Fail: an entry file holding other than one dated `###` heading → `changelog/<file>.md must hold one ### yyyy.mm.dd.HHMM heading...`.\n- Fail: an entry file whose name lacks its heading's date, or carries another → `changelog/<file>.md must be named changelog/<yyyy-mm-dd-HHMM>-<change>.md...`.\n\n## Entry files\n\nA pull request adds its entry as its own `changelog/<yyyy-mm-dd-HHMM>-<change>.md` file, with the change in kebab-case. So two open pull requests never edit the same lines. It holds one `### yyyy.mm.dd.HHMM` heading and its bullets. The name's date is that heading's, dots read as hyphens, so the folder lists in release order.\n\n- A pull request adds exactly one entry file, and later commits add bullets to it.\n- A repo with no `changelog/` folder needs no setting: its first pull request creates it.\n- `changelog-updated`, `pr-check`, and `branch-check` accept only entry files, and fail an added one with a bad name.\n- You write the heading's time by hand; no hook rewrites it.\n- Entry files stay in the folder after a release.\n- An entry file a release tag holds is final. Checks fail an edit or deletion of it, asking for a new entry file.\n- Each release tag's GitHub description lists the entry files added since the previous tag, newest first, as `fitness-release release-notes` writes it.\n",
	"changelog-bullets":            "Keeps changelog entries tight: every [entry file](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-changelog/README.md#entry-files)'s `###` section must have 3 to 5 bullets, each under 365 characters. Each bullet starts with a semantic type, as `Feat:` or `feat(scope):`.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Every entry file is judged, and errors name that file.\n- A pull request adds one dated entry, and later commits amend it, so its 3 to 5 bullets cover the whole PR.\n- Bullet count must be 3 to 5, counting only `- ` bullets; continuation lines join their bullet for the length count.\n- Every bullet must start with one of: `Feat:`, `Fix:`, `Docs:`, `Style:`, `Refactor:`, `Perf:`, `Test:`, `Build:`, `Ci:`, `Chore:`, `Revert:`.\n- A semantic commit subject works too: a lowercase type with an optional scope and `!`, such as `feat(release): ` or `fix!: `.\n- `CHANGELOG.md` is not judged, so a repo with no entry files passes with zero files checked.\n\n## Errors\n\n```\nchangelog/2026-05-01-0000-readme.md section \"2026.05.01.0000\" has 1 bullets; keep entries to 3-5 bullets\nchangelog/2026-05-01-0000-readme.md:7: bullet is 1230 characters; keep each under 365\nchangelog/2026-05-01-0000-readme.md:8: bullet must start with a semantic type (Feat:, Fix:, ...)\n```\n",
	"changelog-updated":            "Checks that each staged commit writes a changelog entry about its change. It runs when the runner provides staged file context. The added lines in the branch's [entry file](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-changelog/README.md#entry-files) must share at least three words with the rest of the staged diff.\n\n## One entry per branch\n\nA branch keeps one entry file, so later commits add bullets to the file its first commit wrote. You write its `### yyyy.mm.dd.HHMM` heading; the [changelog](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-changelog/README.md) check judges its format.\n\n`CHANGELOG.md` is not judged: a commit may add, edit, or delete it, and it counts as part of the rest of the diff.\n\nA branch's own commits are those no other `origin` branch holds, so a stacked branch starts where its base ends. On the default branch, or without `origin` refs, each commit is judged alone. `fitness pr-check` holds the whole pull request to one entry.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass (replay in progress): mid-merge, mid-cherry-pick, or mid-revert (`MERGE_HEAD`, `CHERRY_PICK_HEAD`, or `REVERT_HEAD` present) skips.\n- Pass: no staged context skips.\n- Pass: entry file additions share at least 3 words with the rest of the staged diff.\n- Fail: no staged entry file → \"Stage a changelog/\\<yyyy-mm-dd-HHMM>-\\<change>.md entry file that mentions your staged changes\".\n- Fail: the branch already has its entry file and the commit adds another → error naming the branch's entry file and each new one.\n- Fail: a staged new entry file whose name lacks its heading's date → error naming the file it should be.\n- Fail: a staged edit or deletion of an entry file the newest release tag holds → error asking for a new entry file.\n- Fail: entry file additions share fewer than three words with the rest of the diff → error with the count.\n\nA replay skips, since it re-stages historical entries. A too-small overlap adds a second line, \"use words like: …\", listing up to 10 words from the staged diff. Only the added parts of the entry file in the diff count, with words lowercased and at least 3 long.\n",
	"checkstyle":                   "Lints Java with the [Checkstyle](https://checkstyle.org) plugin the repo's own build declares, and holds its config to a complexity ceiling. The Java language module owns the build run, the ceiling rule and the judgment. It is on by default.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Runs once from each build root, main and test sources alike. Maven runs `checkstyle:check` with warnings counted as violations.\n- Gradle runs `checkstyleMain` and `checkstyleTest` with `--rerun`, since an up-to-date task prints no warnings.\n- Pass: no finding, warnings included.\n- Fail: each finding, as `file:line[:col] - message (Rule)`.\n- A failure that names no finding reports the build's own error lines.\n- No Maven or Gradle build in the repo: passes clean with `filesChecked: 0`.\n- A Java repo with no running JDK fails with an install hint.\n- A build that does not declare Checkstyle fails with the plugin snippet to add.\n\n## Complexity ceiling\n\nEvery tracked Checkstyle config must hold the `CyclomaticComplexity` module, which caps each method's branches. Configs in a test source set's folder, such as `src/test/resources`, are test inputs and left out. A repo with no config fails asking for `config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml`, with the document type line Maven's plugin needs to read it. The hints suggest a `max` of 5; the repo's config sets its own.\n\nMaven runs with the repo's only config, or else with `config/checkstyle/checkstyle.xml`, unless the build names its own.\n",
	"comment-structure":            "Judges one GitHub issue or PR comment, so a reply stays short, readable, and free of agent noise. It holds a word budget outside tables, code, and diagrams, and allows at most one plain table. Diagrams are numbered, and change-log notes, agent frames, and model identifiers fail. It is driven by `fitness comment-check`, locally on a draft and in the [comment-check workflow](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/.github/workflows/README.md#comment-check).\n\nA comment is not a file in the tree. This check reads it from stdin, a `--body-file` path (`-` meaning stdin), or the runner's context-inline `--body` value. With no input it passes with zero files checked. Under `fitness --blob` it skips Plan, Epic, and PR bodies, which have their own structure checks.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Fail: more words than the budget outside tables, code, and diagrams. Headings, HTML comments, links' targets, and URLs do not count.\n- Fail: more plain tables than the budget. A callout table belongs to its diagram and does not count.\n- Fail: a mermaid diagram with no numbered callouts, unless it is the legend right after a numbered diagram.\n- Fail: a change-log note, such as `Update:`, `(edited …)`, or `replaces my earlier`. Edit the text in place instead.\n- Fail: an agent frame, such as a `<system-reminder>` tag, anywhere in the comment, code included.\n- Fail: a fitness marker outside code. Only the known bots post them, and comment-check judges a marker from anyone else.\n- Fail: a model identifier, such as `claude-…` or `gpt-…`, outside code spans, fences, and URLs. Name the agent instead.\n\nEach error names its line. The callout table's numbers are matched by `mermaid-callouts`, and diagrams are parsed by `mermaid-syntax`.\n\nThe budget is 100 words and 1 table, which the `comment` keys below replace when positive:\n\n```\n{\n  \"comment\": {\n    \"maxWords\": 120\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nThese `.fitnessrc.json` keys tune the check.\n\n`comment` holds comment-structure's budget for an issue or PR comment. Each limit falls back to a built-in default when zero.\n\n- `maxWords` is the most words one comment may hold.\n- `maxTables` is the most tables one comment may hold.\n",
	"commit-attribution":           "Requires a commit message to disclose AI use in \"AI-Tools:\" and \"AI-Models:\" git trailers. Each must be present with a non-empty value on its own line after the subject line.\n\nIt applies only to repos that use the convention: the message being checked, or one of the last 100 commits, carries either trailer. Elsewhere it passes clean with zero files.\n\n## Adopt\n\nAdd both trailers to a commit, and every later commit must carry them too. Wire the check into a commit-msg hook so a message is validated before the commit lands:\n\n```\n# .git/hooks/commit-msg\nfitness commit-attribution --message=\"$(cat \"$1\")\"\n```\n\n## What passes\n\nBoth trailers, each on its own line with a non-empty value, appear anywhere in the body in any order. Other trailers may sit alongside:\n\n```\nfeat(api): add pagination to the search endpoint\n\nSupport cursor-based paging so large result sets stay fast.\n\nAI-Tools: Claude Code\nAI-Models: Opus 4.8\nCo-Authored-By: A. Dev <a@dev.io>\n```\n\n## What fails\n\nOne error per missing trailer. This body has neither:\n\n```\nfeat(api): add pagination\n\nJust a body, no attribution.\n```\n\nIt fails with:\n\n```\ncommit message missing \"AI-Models:\" trailer\ncommit message missing \"AI-Tools:\" trailer\n```\n\nA trailer with an empty value does not count as present. An empty message (no subject) fails with one guidance error naming both trailers.\n\n## Advanced\n\nMerge and revert commits are exempt — the subject prefix is matched literally:\n\n```\nMerge branch 'feature' into main    → exempt\nRevert \"feat(api): add endpoint\"    → exempt\n```\n\nDetection is per-line via `^<Key>:[ \\t]*(\\S.*)$`, so an `AI-Tools:` mid-sentence is not mistaken for a trailer. To change the required keys, edit the check and its tests.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Declares `--message` as its context-inline argument, mirroring `semantic-commit`.\n- The runner forwards a proposed message, such as a commit-msg hook's, as `FITNESS_CTX_MESSAGE`; else the check reads the last commit.\n- A message that is provided but empty fails rather than falling back to `git log`.\n- Pass: both trailers present with non-empty values, or a `Merge ` or `Revert ` subject.\n- Fail: one error per missing trailer; on no repo, git error, or empty message, the empty-message guidance error.\n- `filesChecked` is always 1.\n",
	"cspell":                       "Fails unknown words in the checked files. It spell-checks with a native Go engine and dictionaries embedded in the check binary — no external cspell tool, no npm install. The project `cspell.json` words always win over the embedded base dictionaries; it ignores no path.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: no unknown words in checked files.\n- In a scoped run: only the changed paths that still exist on disk are checked; with none left it passes without scanning.\n- Otherwise: spell-checks every tracked file; binary files, with a NUL in their first 8 KB, count but are never spelled or read whole.\n- Generated files: files `.gitattributes` marks `linguist-generated`, and the [lockfiles and build wrapper files](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/lang.go) of any language, are left out; nothing else is.\n- Fail: a resolved `cspell.json` that sets `ignorePaths` fails before any scan; remove it and fix the words instead.\n- Config: a repo-local `cspell.json` wins; otherwise an installed `@mayjournal/fitness-shared` package under `node_modules`; otherwise the copy embedded in the binary, materialized on demand.\n- No copy is required in the consumer repo, so a repo with no npm anywhere still gets the shared words.\n- Forbidden words: each `flagWords` word or phrase fails wherever it appears whole, in any case, except in a `cspell.json` file.\n\nErrors are reported as one line per issue: `path:line:col - Unknown word (word)` or `path:line:col - Forbidden word (entry)`.\n\nA file over 64 KB is checked in line-aligned chunks in parallel, once its `cspell:` directives are applied to the whole file.\n",
	"dependency-currency":          "Fails a declared npm dependency that is behind its latest published version — an installable update means the project isn't at peak fitness. It runs in every repo and passes clean without a `package.json`.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Queries the npm registry over HTTPS (`dist-tags` latest) for each dependency declared in the root `package.json` and its workspaces — no npm binary.\n- Direct declared deps only, since you can only bump what you declare.\n- The registry URL comes from the nearest `.npmrc` `registry=` entry (repo root, then home), defaulting to the public registry.\n- Pass: every dependency is at its latest published version.\n- Fail: one or more dependencies are behind — reports `name: current → latest` per dep, and identical lines collapse to one.\n- A declared-but-uninstalled dep reads as `name: missing → latest`.\n- Internal `@mayjournal/*` workspace packages are skipped, since they are versioned in-repo, not published to the registry.\n- Registry unreachable, or any garbage response: contributes nothing, so a run offline or in CI without network passes.\n\nOne proxy blocks GitHub and the registry alike. So when the runner's probe finds GitHub's API host unreachable, the check reads no registry and reports info naming the host and proxy.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `filesChecked` counts the tracked `package.json` manifests.\n- Reports against `latest`, not `wanted`: a dependency inside its declared semver range but behind the newest release is still flagged.\n- The point is to stay current, not merely in-range.\n",
	"doc-template":                 "Makes each markdown document match the shape of a template that governs it. A template file defines a markdown document's shape, and its neighbors must match it. A folder may hold several templates, one per kind of doc. This is the file-level counterpart of `plan-structure` and `pr-structure`, sharing the `mdtemplate` engine.\n\n## What is a template\n\n- `template.md` or `*.template.md` — a template that governs its neighbors.\n- Any `*.md` directly in a `templates` subfolder — a template for that subfolder's parent folder.\n- A GitHub PR or Issue template under `.github` (`PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`, `ISSUE_TEMPLATE/*.md`).\n\n## Rules\n\n- Every `##` section of a template must carry a guiding `<!-- comment -->`.\n- A folder's templates are its own `template.md`-style files plus every file in its `templates` subfolder.\n- Walking up from each file's own folder, the first folder with a template that governs it decides.\n- The file must have the same `##` sections as any one of that folder's templates.\n- So `docs/arch/adr/0001-x.md` follows `docs/arch/adr/template.md`, while `docs/arch/04-code.md` follows `docs/arch/templates/*.md`.\n- A GitHub template gets the comment rule only; its body is checked by `pr-structure` and `plan-structure`.\n\n## Named templates\n\nA file in a `templates` folder governs alone the neighbor that shares its name, so `docs/architecture/templates/04-code.md` judges `docs/architecture/04-code.md`. It governs no other doc, so the other neighbors match the folder's other templates. A neighbor in a folder of only named templates follows the nearest folder above with others, so `docs/architecture/notes.md` follows `docs/template.md`. A named template holds its namesake to its whole shape:\n\n- Sections: the template's `##` sections open the doc, in order. Sections of the doc's own may follow.\n- Legends: a section with a legend in the template needs a legend of the same Mermaid type.\n- Legend kinds: a diagram with no callout numbers, one titled `Legend`, or one under a `Legend` heading.\n- Diagram types: each diagram and legend in a section has the Mermaid type and direction of the template's there.\n- Callout tables: a section with one in the template needs one, and each has the template's columns.\n- Captions: the paragraph above each callout table starts with the first sentence of the template's caption.\n- Palette: see below, for templates with C4 or class diagrams.\n\n## Palette\n\nA template's C4 and `classDiagram` diagrams, legends included, set its palette: the element kinds they use, and the one style each kind carries. Db and Queue forms count as their base kind, and `Person_Ext` as Person. Every C4 or class diagram in the doc must then:\n\n- Use only those kinds, so a Container in the system context or a `System_Boundary` in the components view fails.\n- Give each element of a styled kind exactly its kind's style: an `UpdateElementStyle` line in C4, a `style` line for a class.\n- Write that style in any order, spacing and case.\n- Leave each unstyled kind, such as a boundary, without style.\n- Hold no other style: another `Update…Style` macro, a color argument, `classDef`, `cssClass`, `:::`, or `%%{init}`. `UpdateLayoutConfig` only lays out, so it stays.\n\nThe journey template draws a flowchart, so its palette judges nothing.\n\n## Shipped templates\n\nFitness ships the five architecture view templates inside the binary. A view under `docs/architecture/` or `architecture/` follows its shipped template, such as [04-code.md](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/architecture/templates/04-code.md), with every rule above. A repo's own copy in the views' `templates` folder governs instead, as a named template. The [architecture-contract README](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-architecture-contract/README.md#templates) lists each level's form and palette.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: every template guides each section, and every governed file matches. A repo with no templates passes.\n- Fail: a template section lacks a comment, or a governed file matches none of its templates.\n- With one template, each difference is its own error. With several, one error names them all.\n",
	"epic-structure":               "Validates that an epic follows the [Epic issue template](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/epic.md), or the repo's own template labeled `Epic`. An epic is a GitHub Issue body under the `Epic` label. Required: a one-line blockquote pitch, a `## Background` section, and a `## What needs to happen` list of issue references in run order. No other `##` sections are allowed.\n\nEpics live as GitHub Issues, so this check reads its target from stdin (or a `--body-file` path, `-` meaning stdin). It falls back to the runner's context-inline `--body` value. With no input at all it passes with zero files checked. It is driven by `fitness issue-check` in the [issue-check workflow](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/.github/workflows/README.md), not the local suite.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: each list item is a plain `#N` or `org/repo#N` reference, with an optional short note after a comma, such as `#12, after #11`.\n- Fail: a checkbox, an emoji, a status word such as done or blocked, or an item that is not a plain reference.\n- Fail: a reference listed twice, or an item listed before an item its `after` note names.\n- Fail: a table anywhere in the body.\n- A list over the `maxListItems` cap fails prose-budget, which issue-check runs on every body; `###` headings split it into shorter lists.\n\nFenced code is skipped, so a `##` or list item inside a code sample never counts. A bare `#N` is an issue in the judged repo, so `#12` and `org/repo#12` match.\n\nChecks never call GitHub, so `fitness issue-check` compares the list with the epic's sub-issues. Every listed issue must be a sub-issue and exist, and every sub-issue must be listed.\n",
	"eslint":                       "Runs ESLint over the repo with the shared flat config, by executing the real [ESLint](https://eslint.org) binary. It resolves the binary from `node_modules/.bin`, walking up from the repo root, then PATH — never npx. When no eslint binary resolves, the check fails with a one-line install hint. The shared flat config `eslint.config.mjs` is forced via `--config`.\n\nThe config resolves in order: the repo's own file, an installed `@mayjournal/fitness-shared` package under `node_modules`, then the copy embedded in the check binary. That embedded copy is materialized on demand.\n\nThe shared config imports its plugins (typescript-eslint, jsdoc, perfectionist, prettier) as bare specifiers. Those packages must be installed alongside eslint in the consumer repo — the check's peer contract. Linting is a syntactic parse only, with no type-aware rules, so the runner-default timeout stands.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: no errors in checked files.\n- Execs eslint with `cwd` set to the repo root, forcing the resolved shared config via `--config`.\n- In a scoped run: only changed paths are linted, passing with 0 files when none is lintable. Otherwise lints every tracked lintable file.\n- Lints `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.cjs`, `.js`, `.mjs`, declarations and tests included.\n- Fail: a tracked file ESLint reports as ignored fails; remove the ignore pattern so it is linted.\n- Enforces `sort-keys` (natural ascending) and other rules from `eslint.config.mjs`.\n\nErrors are reported as `path:line:col - message (ruleId)`.\n\n## Biome\n\nA repo that chose Biome lints with it instead, as [ADR 0006](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/fitness/adr-0006/README.md) records. Such a repo has a `biome.json` or `biome.jsonc` at its root and `@biomejs/biome` in its `package.json` dependencies. The check runs `biome lint --reporter=github` on the same paths, and needs no eslint.\n\nBiome resolves like eslint, and a missing binary fails with an install hint. Biome before 1.8 fails naming its version, since its CLI rejects the GitHub reporter. Each Biome error or warning fails as `path:line:col - message (rule)`.\n",
	"gitignore-why":                "Requires every .gitignore pattern to follow a `#` comment explaining why it exists. This keeps ignores explained and easy to audit and onboard. It is on by default.\n\n## What passes\n\nEvery pattern has a `#` comment on the line directly above it:\n\n```\n# Installed dependencies, never committed\nnode_modules/\n\n# Build output, regenerated by `npm run build`\ndist/\n\n# Local env secrets\n.env\n```\n\nThe rule is strict: one comment per pattern, on the line immediately above. Two patterns under one heading each need their own comment — a single comment does not cover both:\n\n```\n# Editor swap files\n*.swp\n# macOS folder metadata\n.DS_Store\n```\n\nBlank lines and a file-level header comment are fine as long as each pattern still has an explanatory comment directly above it.\n\n## What fails\n\nBare patterns with no explanation:\n\n```\nnode_modules/\ndist/\n.env\n```\n\nproduce one error per pattern:\n\n```\n.gitignore:1: pattern \"node_modules/\" has no explanatory # comment on the line above\n.gitignore:2: pattern \"dist/\" has no explanatory # comment on the line above\n.gitignore:3: pattern \".env\" has no explanatory # comment on the line above\n```\n\nOther failures include a bare `#` (no text) above a pattern, another pattern directly above, or a pattern as the very first line. A comment not directly above the pattern also fails — the blank line breaks the pairing, so line 3 is flagged:\n\n```\n# Dependencies\n\nnode_modules/\n```\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Reads `<root>/.gitignore`. When the file is absent, passes with `filesChecked` 0.\n- Classifies each line as blank, a `#` comment, or a pattern (any other non-blank line, including `!` negations and `dir/` rules).\n- Pass: every pattern line is directly preceded by a non-empty explanatory `#` comment.\n- Fail: reports `.gitignore:<lineNumber>: pattern \"<text>\" has no explanatory # comment on the line above` for each offending pattern.\n- `filesChecked` is 1 whenever `.gitignore` is present.\n",
	"go-complexity":                "Enforces a cyclomatic complexity ceiling on Go functions — the Go-native counterpart of the house eslint rule (`complexity: max 5`) that gates JavaScript and TypeScript.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- A function starts at 1 and gains a point per `if`, `for`, `range`, non-default `switch` or `select` case, `&&`, and `||`.\n- Function literals score separately from their enclosing function, the way eslint scores arrow functions.\n- Test files (`_test.go`) are scored like any other Go file; table-driven tests keep them under the ceiling.\n- A file that fails to parse fails the check with the parser's error.\n- `filesChecked` counts every Go file scanned, tests included.\n\n## Errors\n\n```\ngo/internal/example/thing.go:42: func parseAll has a complexity of 8; maximum allowed is 5\n```\n\nFunction literals report as `function literal`; methods report as `method <name>`.\n\n## Ceiling\n\nThe ceiling defaults to 5. Override it per repo in `.fitnessrc.json`:\n\n```\n{\n  \"goComplexity\": { \"max\": 8 }\n}\n```\n\nA native Go engine (`go/ast` and `go/parser` from the standard library) scores the code; no external tool is required.\n\n## Configuration\n\nThese `.fitnessrc.json` keys tune the check.\n\n`goComplexity` holds options for the go-complexity check.\n\n- `max` is the highest cyclomatic complexity one Go function may have.\n",
	"go-test":                      "Runs `go test ./...` in every Go module of the repo and reports the failing tests. It is on by default.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Finds each `go.mod` in the repo, leaving out `testdata` and `vendor` fixtures and paths the config ignores.\n- Runs `go test ./...` in each module. A failure reports failing tests, panics, build errors, and each test's `file.go:line` messages.\n- A repo with no `go.mod` passes clean (`filesChecked: 0`), so the check is safe in every repo.\n- A run scoped to changed files skips when no `.go`, `go.mod`, or `go.sum` file changed.\n- A missing Go toolchain fails with a one-line install hint.\n- The time budget is 15 minutes, since a test suite can run long.\n",
	"go-test-coverage":             "Measures entry coverage and test overlap. It requires full entry coverage and reports gaps below it. Select this check by name; it is not yet in the default suite.\n\n## Run\n\n```\nfitness go-test-coverage --all\n```\n\nEvery command package is an entry and must reach 100% statement coverage. Declare library entry packages by their full import paths:\n\n```\n{\n  \"goTestCoverage\": {\n    \"entries\": [\"example.com/team/project/api\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA module without commands needs a declared entry. Unknown entries fail. Entries add to discovered commands; they cannot hide a command package.\n\n## Results\n\nThe check prints file, package, and folder counts to standard error. Entries require 100%; other code targets 100% and reports gaps. Exact counts decide failure, even when a displayed percentage rounds to 100.0%.\n\n```\nentry example.com/team/project/cmd/tool: 9/10 statements; required 100%\n```\n\nEntry tests earn coverage in child packages. A child folder does not need separate tests for statements already reached. Untested code remains in the totals. No executable statements means no coverage gap.\n\nThis measures statements, not branches or assertion quality.\n\n## Test overlap\n\nThe audit runs each top-level test and shared setup twice with fresh profiles. Subtests stay with their parent. It subtracts setup coverage before comparing covered blocks. Changes across repeats mark the group as unstable.\n\nReports show test names, the elapsed time of both runs, covered blocks, unique blocks, and identical coverage sets. Unstable sets are flagged and excluded from overlap advice. Tests with no measured production coverage get a separate finding and do not count as identical coverage.\n\nThe audit always runs. Exact and partial overlap remain review signals because different inputs and assertions can legitimately execute the same blocks. A top-level test fails when it passes a named helper to `t.Run`; this prevents hiding an independently measured test behind a parent. The legacy `--audit` flag remains accepted.\n\n## Audit runs\n\nEach package's test binary is built once with the coverage flags. Every run then starts that binary from the package directory, with `FITNESS_GO_COVER_DIR` set. Packages run side by side, up to the runner's own check pool size, and share that many process slots.\n\nWithin a package, setup and serial tests run in sequence, as go test runs them. Tests whose own body calls `t.Parallel()` on their parameter then run alongside each other, unless the package has a `TestMain`. Setup runs again last. A failure stops only later packages, so the earliest failing package is reported.\n\n## Source map\n\nKeep the summary metrics and add line-to-test claims with an explicit export. The map includes test block sets and pairwise intersections, so partial overlap is visible.\n\n```\nAUDIT_DIR=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\nfitness go-test-coverage --all --audit-map=\"$AUDIT_DIR/claims.json\"\n```\n\nThe output path must be new, with an existing parent outside the repository. A completed measurement writes the map even when entry coverage fails.\n\n## Map fields\n\nEach module has numbered blocks and lines, test claims, and overlapping test pairs. Source paths are relative to the module; `sourceFiles` maps profile names to those paths. IDs are local to each module and repeat consistently for the same claims.\n\n- `blocks`: file, line and column range, statement count, test owners, and shared setup owners.\n- `lines`: source line, block IDs, test owners, and shared setup owners.\n- `tests`: test name, stable status, covered block IDs, and setup block IDs.\n- `overlaps`: two test names, shared block IDs, and shared source-line IDs.\n\nGo measures statement blocks. The line map projects their ranges; it does not claim that each physical line ran. Tests can share a line while reaching separate blocks: their shared line list is nonempty and their shared block list is empty.\n\n## Claim rules\n\nOnly stable test contributions count toward pair comparisons. Shared setup is retained with its own owners, and unstable groups retain their status without confirmed claims. Subtests remain grouped under their top-level test.\n\nArray order and IDs are fixed by source positions and test names. The map omits wall-clock timing; durations remain in the existing summary. Pair entries include partial intersections as well as identical sets.\n\n## Compiled tools\n\nTests that launch a Go binary must build it with `go build -cover`. They must set the child's `GOCOVERDIR` to the `FITNESS_GO_COVER_DIR` path supplied by this check. The check merges those measured statements with the test profile. Ordinary builds and child runs without collected data earn no credit.\n\nUse the same source and build flags for test and child coverage. Conflicting counts or unknown source paths fail. Standard library and dependency coverage are outside the module inventory.\n\n## Scope and cost\n\nThe check uses existing Go module discovery and changed-file scope. Each module runs with its own fresh profiles and workspace mode off. Test errors, missing Go, bad profiles, and missing source coverage fail. Repos without Go modules pass.\n\nAll profiles live in private temporary paths and are removed on return. Tests may still write their own files. The audit reruns every top-level test twice, so its cost is about twice the tests' own run time, spread over the pool. The runner allows fifteen minutes; the check stops work after fourteen.\n\n## Rollout\n\nBoth org and external defaults keep their current checks. Select this check explicitly while entry coverage gaps are repaired. Lower-level floors and default adoption need a separate policy decision.\n\n```\nfitness --policy=external --checks=go-test-coverage --all\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nThese `.fitnessrc.json` keys tune the check.\n\n`goTestCoverage` declares library entry packages for the optional go-test-coverage check.\n\n- `entries` lists the full import paths of library entry packages, which must reach full statement coverage, as command packages do.\n",
	"go-vet":                       "Runs `go vet ./...` in every Go module of the repo, catching suspicious code the compiler accepts. It is on by default.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Finds each `go.mod` in the repo, leaving out `testdata` and `vendor` fixtures and paths the config ignores.\n- Runs `go vet ./...` in each module and reports its findings, prefixed with the module directory when it is not the repo root.\n- A repo with no `go.mod` passes clean (`filesChecked: 0`), so the check is safe in every repo.\n- A run scoped to changed files skips when no `.go`, `go.mod`, or `go.sum` file changed.\n- A missing Go toolchain fails with a one-line install hint.\n- `filesChecked` counts the modules vetted.\n",
	"gofmt":                        "Lists Go files that gofmt would change. It is on by default.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Checks the repo's own `.go` files, leaving out `testdata` and `vendor` fixtures and paths the config ignores.\n- Runs `gofmt -l` over them and reports each listed file as `path: not gofmt-formatted (run: gofmt -w path)`.\n- A repo with no `.go` files passes clean (`filesChecked: 0`), so the check is safe in every repo.\n- A missing Go toolchain fails with a one-line install hint.\n- `filesChecked` counts the Go files checked.\n",
	"issue-checklist":              "Fails an issue body whose task list still has unchecked items, so closing it would close unfinished work.\n\nIt reads the body from a `--body-file` path, `-` meaning stdin, or from the runner's context-inline `--body` value. With no input it passes with zero files checked. `fitness close-check` runs it on a closed issue, and `fitness pr-check` on each issue a pull request closes.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: every `- [ ]` item is ticked, or the body has no task list.\n- Fail: one error per unchecked item, quoting its text.\n\nItems inside fenced code are examples, so they never count.\n",
	"issue-link-once":              "Keeps each Issue's timeline readable ([#153](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/153)). Every commit that links an Issue adds an entry there, so a branch links an Issue from one commit at most.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- A link is `#NN`, `owner/repo#NN`, or an Issue or PR URL on github.com. A bare `#NN` belongs to origin's repo.\n- Fail: an earlier commit of the branch's own, one no other pushed branch holds, links the same Issue.\n- A branch stacked on a pushed epic branch so starts where that branch ends.\n- Fail: the open PR body links the Issue, and the commit is newer than the PR.\n- Comment lines and anything below the scissors line are ignored, as git drops them.\n\n## Where it runs\n\nThe `commit-msg` hook judges the proposed message, which it passes as the context-inline `--message` value. An amend leaves out HEAD, the commit it replaces. With no open PR, or no answer from gh, it warns and skips the PR rule. When GitHub's API host misses the runner's probe, it skips only the open PR read, with a note naming the host and proxy.\n\nOn a pull request, the org `fitness-suite` workflow judges every branch commit, oldest first, in every repo ([#157](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/157)). The one link written before the PR opened passes, judged by author date, which a rebase keeps. A failure stays red until you rewrite that commit without the link and force-push with `--force-with-lease`.\n\nAnywhere else, such as a local suite run or a push to `main`, it passes inert.\n",
	"java-test":                    "Runs Java tests once per build root and judges their JUnit XML results and [JaCoCo](https://www.jacoco.org/jacoco/) coverage. The Java language module owns the build run and the judgment. It is on by default.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Maven runs `verify` past failing tests, so Surefire, Failsafe and the JaCoCo reports bound there all run.\n- Gradle cleans and reruns `test` and `jacocoTestReport`, or `testCodeCoverageReport` under report aggregation. Another test task counts when one of those depends on it.\n- Gradle adds `--build-cache`, so unchanged tests and reports are restored, not rerun. Failed tests are never cached.\n- `org.gradle.caching=false` in `gradle.properties` turns the cache off.\n- The [Java module](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/java/java.go) says what a build root is and which wrapper or tool runs it.\n- No Maven or Gradle build in the repo: passes clean with `filesChecked: 0`.\n- A Java repo with no running JDK fails with an install hint.\n- A build that does not declare JaCoCo, or writes no XML report, fails with the plugin snippet to add.\n\n## Findings\n\n- Each failing test, as `Class.method failed: message`, read from the JUnit XML the run wrote.\n- A failed build with no failing test reports the build's own error lines.\n- A build root with product code whose run ran no test. Test files that ran nothing get each detected framework's hint.\n- A JaCoCo line or branch total below the shared goal, as `report: lines covered/total covered; the goal is 100%`.\n- A report that does not parse, or a JaCoCo report that counts no lines.\n- A Docker check that fails or hangs past 10 seconds, for a framework that needs Docker.\n\n## Reports\n\nOnly reports the run itself wrote count. The JUnit XML comes from `target/surefire-reports`, `target/failsafe-reports` and Gradle's `build/test-results/<task>`.\n\nCoverage reads each report's own totals, never a sum of its classes. An aggregate report, Maven's `jacoco-aggregate` or Gradle's `testCodeCoverageReport`, replaces the module reports.\n\n## Frameworks\n\nThe [frameworks](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/java/tests/frameworks) folder holds one commented file per framework, spotted from build files and imports. Each says how its tests reach the JUnit XML. A framework that needs a running service, as Testcontainers needs Docker, fails before the run when the service is down.\n",
	"java-version":                 "Checks a Java repo pins its JDK and that the machine's JDKs satisfy the pin, through the Java language module. It is on by default.\n\n## Pins\n\n- Version files at the repo root, the ones `actions/setup-java` reads: `.java-version`, `.sdkmanrc` (`java=`) and `.tool-versions` (`java`).\n- Maven's `maven.compiler.release` or compiler `release`, one `${property}` deep.\n- A Gradle toolchain's `JavaLanguageVersion.of(N)`.\n\nA pom that names no release takes one from its parents outside the repo, read from the local Maven repository. That is the one `.mvn/maven.config` or `MAVEN_OPTS` sets, else the user settings' `localRepository`, else `~/.m2/repository`. The properties of the pom and its parents in the repo come first, so a parent may map a property the pom sets. A parent the local repository lacks fails, when nothing else pins a release, asking to run the build once.\n\n## Toolchains\n\nA Gradle toolchain asks for its exact release, not a newer one. Gradle always finds the JDK it runs on, so that release passes at once. Any other release must be installed, and the check looks for it without starting Gradle.\n\nThe lookup reads `JAVA_HOME`, macOS's `/usr/libexec/java_home -V`, and Gradle's detection folders. Those are `~/.gradle/jdks`, SDKMAN, asdf, jabba, `~/.jdks`, Homebrew, `/usr/lib/jvm` and the macOS JDK folders, where a bundle keeps `release` under `Contents/Home`. It adds the folders `org.gradle.java.installations.paths` names, and the variables `org.gradle.java.installations.fromEnv` names. Both split on commas, and come from the build root's `gradle.properties`, overridden by the Gradle user home's.\n\nA toolchain that sets `vendor` or J9 needs an installed JDK from it, else the check fails naming the JDK to install. A resolver exempts it, since Gradle then downloads the JDK: a download repository in `toolchainManagement`, or the Foojay plugin. Either may sit in the settings script or a user home init script. Commented-out code does not count, and `org.gradle.java.installations.auto-download=false` turns the exemption off.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: the running JDK's feature release is at least each pinned one, since a newer JDK builds for an older release.\n- Fail: no pin anywhere, with the snippets that add one.\n- Fail: a pin above the running JDK, naming the file and the release it needs.\n- Fail: a pin that names no release.\n- The running JDK is `JAVA_HOME`'s, as the build tools pick it, else `java` on `PATH`.\n- With no running JDK, the check fails with an install hint.\n- No Maven or Gradle build in the repo: passes clean with `filesChecked: 0`.\n",
	"jscpd":                        "Fails a repo whose duplicated lines pass 1% of its scanned lines, with [jscpd](https://github.com/kucherenko/jscpd) semantics. A native Go clone-detection engine runs instead of the jscpd tool: a generic lexer plus a rolling-hash detector in `go/internal/clonedetect`. Its verdict and error format match jscpd's; exact percentage parity with jscpd's per-language tokenizers is not the goal. It is on by default.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Scans every tracked file with min-lines 5, min-tokens 50, tests, Markdown, and JSON included.\n- Binary files are skipped, since they have no lines to compare.\n- Files the repo's `.gitattributes` marks `linguist-generated` are left out.\n- Lockfiles and build wrapper files are left out, as each [language module](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/langs/langs.go) or the [lockfiles](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/lockfiles) folder names them.\n- Pass: duplicated lines stay under 1% of the codebase, with jscpd's threshold semantics.\n- Fail: over threshold — the check reports `ERROR: jscpd found too many duplicates (X%) over threshold (Y%)`, then `path: N duplicated lines` for each file holding them.\n- The engine is built into the check binary — no external jscpd tool to install.\n\n## Templates\n\nA doc must repeat its template, so templates are left out. Any file [doc-template](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-doc-template/README.md#what-is-a-template) treats as a template is skipped, in any folder. Docs built from one template are still compared with each other and with every other file.\n\n## Escape hatch\n\nFor duplication that's a real, justified constraint, such as boilerplate a framework requires per type, wrap it in jscpd's inline-comment markers. Don't force a refactor:\n\n```\n// jscpd:ignore-start\n...\n// jscpd:ignore-end\n```\n",
	"local-branch":                 "Runs PR and Plan judgments locally. It runs the existing PR and Plan checks against this branch's live GitHub metadata, without posting comments or changing issues.\n\n## Usage\n\n```\nfitness local-branch\nfitness branch-check\nfitness --policy=external --checks=local-branch\n```\n\nFull local Fitness checks the current branch. Pre-push runs one meta-check over each live pushed destination branch and local tip, including branches outside the checkout. Deletions and tags skip; pre-commit excludes it. In Actions or a repo without a GitHub origin, it passes without network reads.\n\n## Behavior\n\nThe check matches the current branch's open PR, including drafts and forks, then validates its closing issues and branch Plan trailers. History starts at the fetched base branch when it contains the API base commit, else at that commit. Upstream Plans and entries merged since never count. Ordinary issues receive PR checklist rules.\n\nNo PR or detached HEAD skips. Ambiguous PRs, unfinished checklists, inaccessible metadata, and missing base history fail with retry guidance.\n\nGitHub reads share a 30-second deadline; the runner allows 35 seconds for cancellation and reporting. Inside pre-push, the gate opens the push's deadline before its Plan reads and hands it over. This check then reads under what is left of it. When the runner's two-second probe finds GitHub's API host unreachable, the check reads nothing and reports info naming the host and proxy.\n\n## Implementation\n\n`go/internal/metadata` supplies the same judgments to this check and the Actions commands. Only Actions applies GitHub effects. The meta-check calls judgments directly, so it cannot recursively run the suite.\n",
	"localized-text":               "Fails SwiftUI text and requirement test literals that skip the app's [String Catalog](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/localizing-and-varying-text-with-a-string-catalog) ([#527](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/527)). The Swift language module owns the reading and its judgment. A literal passed to `Text` compiles as a [LocalizedStringKey](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/localizedstringkey), so a missing entry only shows in another language. Requirements: [0097](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/docs/requirements/0097-checks-localized-text.md).\n\n## Views\n\nAn app opts in by tracking a `.xcstrings` file. Without one the check passes with zero files.\n\n- Text views: `Text`, `Label`, `Button`, `Section`, `Link` and `ProgressView`.\n- Input views: `TextField`, `SecureField`, `Toggle`, `Picker` and `Menu`.\n- Container views: `LabeledContent`, `DisclosureGroup`, `GroupBox` and `ContentUnavailableView`.\n- Title modifiers: `navigationTitle`, `navigationBarTitle`, `help` and `badge`.\n- Other modifiers: `accessibilityLabel`, `accessibilityHint`, `alert` and `confirmationDialog`.\n- A `verbatim:` argument counts too.\n- A reference such as `Text(Words.Verb.share)`, a value, a labeled argument such as `systemImage: \"photo\"`, and a literal without letters pass.\n\nA failure reads:\n\n```\nApp/ShareView.swift:7 - \"Share\" passed to Button skips the String Catalog; use a Words reference for catalog entry \"verb.share\"\n```\n\n## Expressions\n\n- A literal that is a branch of a ternary or `??` expression in a view's first argument fails.\n- A ternary branch inside a literal's interpolation fails. Another literal there, such as a dictionary key, passes.\n- A multi-line `\"\"\"` literal is one literal and is named whole.\n- A test literal assigned with `=` or passed under an argument label, such as `caption: \"Share\"`, is plain data and passes.\n\n## Tests\n\n- A test file sits in a folder whose name ends in `Tests`, or is named `…Tests.swift`.\n- A test literal fails when it equals an entry's source-language words, plural and device forms included.\n- Stale entries, entries marked not to translate, and the empty key are no labels.\n- So test captions, prices, symbol names, and checks that a removed word is gone pass.\n\nEach failure names the file, line, and literal, the call or catalog entry, and the reference to use. In a pull request, or a staged commit, it judges only the changed Swift files against every catalog.\n\n## Reference and allow list\n\n`localizedText.reference` names the type views and tests read entries through, as failures name it. Without it they say `String(localized:)`.\n\n`localizedText.allow` passes a literal its `pattern` regular expression matches whole. Each pattern needs a `reason`, and one without, or one that does not compile, fails the check:\n\n```\n{\n  \"localizedText\": {\n    \"reference\": \"Words\",\n    \"allow\": [{ \"pattern\": \"Beta [0-9]+\", \"reason\": \"build tags stay in English for testers\" }]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOrg repos cannot turn the check off, as [ADR 0009](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/fitness/adr-0009/README.md) decides.\n\n## Configuration\n\nThese `.fitnessrc.json` keys tune the check.\n\n`localizedText` holds the localized-text check's options.\n\n- `reference` names the type views and tests read catalog entries through, such as Words.\n- `allow` lists literals the check passes, each with its reason.\n- `allow.pattern` is a regular expression a literal must match whole.\n- `allow.reason` says why the literal stays out of the catalog.\n",
	"markdown-filename-camel-case": "Requires every markdown file name to be camelCase, standard root docs exempt. The validation core is shared with the kebab-case flavor in `go/internal/mdfilename`; see [markdown-filename-kebab-case](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-markdown-filename-kebab-case/README.md) for behavior and conventions covering both flavors.\n\nThis flavor pins the camelCase convention: every `.md` basename must match `^[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9]*\\.md$`, standard root docs and names GitHub requires exempt.\n",
	"markdown-filename-kebab-case": "Requires every markdown file name to be kebab-case, standard root docs exempt. So doc trees stay predictable for links, tooling, and case-sensitive CI. Standard root docs (`README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, `LICENSE.md`, `SECURITY.md`) are always exempt.\n\nTwo check binaries share one validation core (`go/internal/mdfilename`) — two flavors of the same fitness function, each pinned to one convention:\n\n- `markdown-filename-kebab-case` — basename must be kebab-case: `^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*\\.md$`\n- `markdown-filename-camel-case` — basename must be camelCase: `^[a-z][a-zA-Z0-9]*\\.md$`\n\nBoth run in every repo, but only the repo's own convention judges. Hyphenated names vote kebab-case, names with capitals vote camelCase, and the majority wins; kebab-case wins a tie.\n\n## What passes\n\n`markdown-filename-kebab-case` — lowercase alphanumeric words joined by single hyphens:\n\n```\ndocs/api-design.md\narchitecture/02-containers.md\nadr001.md\n```\n\n`markdown-filename-camel-case` — lowercase first letter, then letters or digits, no separators:\n\n```\ndocs/releaseNotes.md\ndocs/apiDesign.md\nadr001.md\n```\n\n## What fails\n\nEach flavor rejects anything outside its convention (and always rejects `snake_case`, `PascalCase`, and spaces), reporting the path and the required convention:\n\n```\ndocs/releaseNotes.md: filename must be kebab-case\ndocs/api-design.md: filename must be camelCase\ndocs/Api_Design.md: filename must be kebab-case\n```\n\n## Advanced\n\nNames GitHub requires are exempt too. GitHub reads `pull_request_template.md` and `issue_template.md` in lowercase from the root, `docs`, or `.github`, so a repo cannot rename them. In any other folder, those names are judged like any doc.\n\nOnly the basename is validated, so directory casing is ignored — `SomeDir/api-design.md` is judged solely by `api-design.md`. On case-insensitive filesystems (macOS default), two camelCase names that differ only in letter case resolve to the same file. Prefer `markdown-filename-kebab-case` when that collision risk matters. To keep a non-conforming legacy filename, rename it or add it to the exempt names in `go/internal/mdfilename`.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Discovers every tracked `.md` file; no directory is skipped.\n- Pass: every basename is a standard root doc, a name GitHub requires, or matches the enabled flavor's convention.\n- Fail: `path: filename must be <kebab-case|camelCase>` per non-conforming file.\n- `filesChecked` counts every `.md` file scanned.\n",
	"markdown-links":               "Requires every relative link in every markdown file to resolve to an existing file or directory. Broken relative links survive refactors silently — a docs move or a file deletion leaves pointers that no other check sees. This check makes them commit-blocking.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Inline links, images, and reference-style definitions are checked in every `.md` file.\n- Absolute URLs (any scheme), protocol-relative URLs, and fragment-only links (`#anchor`) are never touched — the check is fully offline and deterministic.\n- A `#fragment` suffix is stripped before resolution; anchor names themselves are not validated.\n- Targets resolve relative to the linking file's directory and may be files or directories. A percent-encoded path (`my%20file.md`) is decoded first.\n- Links inside fenced code blocks and inline code spans are ignored.\n\n## Errors\n\n```\ndocs/competition.md:11: broken relative link: ./README.md\n```\n",
	"markdown-no-bold-italic":      "Fails bold and italic emphasis in markdown files: `**bold**`, `__bold__`, `*italic*`, or `_italic_`.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: no .md files, or no markdown file contains bold (double asterisk or double underscore) or italic (single asterisk or single underscore) emphasis.\n- Fail: any .md file contains disallowed emphasis; one error per occurrence with file path and snippet.\n\nScans every tracked `.md` file, `CHANGELOG.md` included. Use bold or italic only when explicitly required. Ignores emphasis inside fenced code, inline code, and markdown link blocks `[text](url)`, so underscores in URLs or link text are not flagged. The bold scope Release Please gives release bullets is allowed too.\n",
	"mermaid-callout-why":          "Requires each numbered callout table to carry a \"Why\" column, and flags numbered rows whose \"Why\" cell is empty. So tables explain why each element exists, not just what it is. It is part of the mermaid diagram and callout table fitness-function set ([#28](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/28)).\n\n## Behavior\n\nFor each numbered callout table, it requires a `Why` column (case-insensitive) in the header. It warns when a callout row leaves its `Why` cell empty. A callout row opens with a node number, such as `1`, or a connector letter, such as `A`, as [mermaid-callouts](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-mermaid-callouts/README.md) reads them.\n\nIt runs on every `.md` file with a mermaid diagram or callout table; files with neither are skipped and don't count toward `filesChecked`.\n",
	"mermaid-callouts":             "Requires every mermaid diagram with callouts to have a callout table whose callouts match it one to one. Orphan callouts, orphan rows, and duplicates fail. It is the foundational check of the mermaid diagram and callout table fitness-function set ([#28](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/28)).\n\n## Behavior\n\nFor each `.md` file, diagrams (fenced `mermaid` blocks) are paired with the callout table that follows them. That table is a GFM table whose first header cell is `#`, `No`, `Callout`, or `Ref`.\n\nNodes carry number callouts and connectors carry letter callouts, so a reader tells them apart at a glance ([#583](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/583)). Letters run `A` to `Z`, then double: `AA`, `BB`, up to `ZZ`, then `AAA`. The table's `#` column holds the same letters for connector rows.\n\n- Pass: every callout in the diagram maps to exactly one table row, and vice versa. Diagrams without callouts need no table.\n- Fail (orphan callout): `diagram callout N has no matching table row`.\n- Fail (orphan row): `callout table row N has no matching diagram callout`.\n- Fail (duplicate): `diagram callout N appears K times` or `callout table row N appears K times`.\n- Fail (numbered connector): `connector callout N must be a letter; numbers are for nodes`.\n- Fail (broken doubling): `connector callout AB breaks the letter doubling`, or the same for a table row. A label such as YES that no row lists is prose.\n- Fail (unpaired diagram): `mermaid diagram … has callouts but no associated callout table`.\n- Fail (unpaired table): `callout table … has no preceding mermaid diagram`.\n\n## Reading callouts\n\nA node callout is the leading integer of a node label: quoted (`Container(x, \"3\", …)`, `A[\"1 Check\"]`) or inside a node shape (`A[2 Screen]`).\n\nA connector callout is the leading label of an edge, in any of these forms:\n\n- C4 relationship: `Rel(a, b, \"A\")`.\n- Flowchart pipe: `a -->|B| b` or `a -->|\"B\"| b`.\n- Inline link: `a -- C --> b`.\n- Colon after an arrow: `a --> b : D` or `a ->> b: D`.\n\nA colon label with no arrow before it, such as a state description or a note, is a node's. Capital letters that break the doubling count only when they are the whole label, so prose such as `|API calls|` is not a callout. Such a label also never makes a diagram need a table on its own. In the table, callouts come from the first (`#`) column: a number, capital letters, or either in brackets.\n\n## Scope\n\nRuns on every `.md` file that contains a diagram or callout table. Files with neither are skipped and don't count toward `filesChecked`.\n",
	"mermaid-diagram-prose":        "Fails edge labels that carry prose beyond a callout, a number or a letter, in a diagram paired with a callout table. Diagram labels must not duplicate callout table prose. It is part of the mermaid diagram and callout table fitness-function set ([#28](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/28)).\n\n## Behavior\n\nIn a diagram paired with a callout table, it flags edge labels with prose after a callout number or letter. For example, `\"6 Uses\"` should be `\"6\"` and `\"A calls\"` should be `\"A\"`. Arrows carry callouts only; descriptions belong in the table, not the diagram.\n\nIt runs on every `.md` file with a mermaid diagram or callout table; files with neither are skipped and don't count toward `filesChecked`.\n",
	"mermaid-diagram-table-gap":    "Flags loose markdown prose wedged between a numbered mermaid diagram (or its legend block) and the callout table that follows it. It is part of the mermaid diagram and callout table fitness-function set ([#28](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/28)). The callout table is the single home for detail ([#66](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/66)).\n\n## Behavior\n\nThe C4 doc layout is diagram → legend → one caption line → callout table, with every description in the table. This flags any non-blank markdown line in the gap between the table and the last mermaid block before it. That block is the legend when present, else the diagram. Such a paragraph just duplicates the table.\n\nThe single caption line is allowed: a line that opens with `Numbers` and states they `match the callout table`. Any wording in between and any trailing clause after is fine. Prose before the diagram and prose after the table are both left alone — only the diagram-to-table gap is policed.\n\nA sibling check, `mermaid-diagram-prose`, owns the different rule of prose inside the diagram's own labels. This check never looks inside a block; it looks at the markdown lines around them.\n\n## Scope\n\nIt runs on every `.md` file with a mermaid diagram or callout table; files with neither are skipped and don't count toward `filesChecked`.\n",
	"mermaid-legend":               "Requires numbered flowchart callout nodes to carry a style class, and diagrams with callouts to declare a `classDef` legend. It is part of the mermaid diagram and callout table fitness-function set ([#28](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/28)).\n\n## Behavior\n\nA class is inline `:::class` or a `class` or `style` statement. The check flags numbered callout nodes with no styling, since consistent styling keeps callouts legible in a legend. C4 and other diagram types, with their different styling model, are skipped.\n\nConnectors carry letter callouts, not numbers, as [mermaid-callouts](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-mermaid-callouts/README.md) requires. Links take `linkStyle`, not a class, so this check skips connector letters.\n\nIt runs on every `.md` file with a mermaid diagram or callout table; files with neither are skipped and don't count toward `filesChecked`.\n",
	"mermaid-level-bleed":          "Warns when a callout description at one C4 level repeats the previous level verbatim. Lower levels should add level-specific rationale, not restate the parent. It is part of the mermaid diagram and callout table fitness-function set ([#28](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/28)).\n\n## Behavior\n\nAcross numbered `architecture/NN-*.md` files, it compares callout descriptions at adjacent levels. It warns when a lower level repeats an upper level verbatim, adding nothing new. It compares only exact, normalized matches, to stay low on false positives.\n\n`filesChecked` counts the numbered level files, whether or not they contain mermaid blocks.\n",
	"mermaid-syntax":               "Fails a mermaid diagram that would not render, so a broken diagram never reaches a reader. It is part of the mermaid diagram and callout table fitness-function set ([#28](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/28)).\n\n## Behavior\n\nThe check parses each fenced `mermaid` block in pure Go, with no npm or browser. Errors name the document line and the diagram's opening fence.\n\n- Flowcharts and graphs: the header, node shapes, edges and their labels, `&` groups, `subgraph` blocks, and styling statements.\n- An unquoted flowchart label holding brackets or quotes fails; wrap it in double quotes.\n- Sequence diagrams: participants, notes, messages with a target and a colon, and blocks with their branches and `end`.\n- Other known types, such as `C4Context` and `classDiagram`: balanced brackets and quotes only, since a full grammar for each is out of reach.\n- `erDiagram`, `mindmap`, and `block-beta`: the header only, since their syntax leaves brackets unpaired.\n- An unknown diagram type fails.\n\nIt runs on every `.md` file with a mermaid diagram and, given `--body-file`, on one Plan, PR, or comment body. Files with no diagram are skipped and don't count toward `filesChecked`.\n",
	"no-contrastive-reframing":     "Flags the \"not X, it's Y\" reframing in markdown prose, where a sentence rejects a claim and then restates the real point. An example is `It's not a workout. It's a lifestyle.` It favors precision over recall, so it fires narrowly and leaves ordinary negations alone.\n\n## What it flags\n\n- The split form: a demonstrative negation followed by a demonstrative assertion (`It's not a workout. It's a lifestyle.`).\n- The single-sentence form: a demonstrative negation with a contrast pivot (`It's not a workout, but a lifestyle.`).\n\n## What it leaves alone\n\n- A sentence that does not open with `It's`, `That's`, `This is`, or a kindred copula.\n- An ordinary negation with no restatement, and the additive `not only X but also Y`.\n- Fenced code, headings, tables, inline code, and quoted spans, via the shared `mdx` prose masking, so documenting the pattern does not self-flag.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: no prose sentence matches, or the match sits in code or quotes.\n- Fail: a sentence matches one of the two forms — one error per match, quoting the snippet.\n\nThe detection is deliberately narrow; widen it only with tests that pin the false-positive line.\n",
	"no-eslint-disable":            "Fails any JS or TS source file that holds an ESLint disable directive, in file, block, `-line`, or `-next-line` form. This keeps lint hygiene on the fix-the-rule path instead of accumulating suppressions. It is on by default.\n\n## What passes\n\nA source file with no disable directives:\n\n```\n// src/slug.ts\nexport function toSlug(input: string): string {\n  return input.trim().toLowerCase().replace(/\\s+/g, '-');\n}\n```\n\n## What fails\n\nAny of the directive forms — file-level, block, line, or next-line:\n\n```\n// src/legacy.ts\nexport const x = 1;\n// eslint-disable-next-line no-console\nconsole.log(x);\nconst y = 2; // eslint-disable-line\n/* eslint-disable */\n```\n\nproduces one error per hit, with the matched directive and its 1-based line number:\n\n```\nsrc/legacy.ts:3: eslint-disable-next-line\nsrc/legacy.ts:5: eslint-disable-line\nsrc/legacy.ts:6: eslint-disable\n```\n\n## Advanced\n\nDetection is a plain line scan (grep-like), so the directive text is flagged even inside a string or unrelated comment:\n\n```\n// Flagged, even though it only describes the directive:\nconst hint = 'prefix a line with eslint-disable-next-line to skip a rule';\n```\n\nReword the rare case rather than suppressing it. Scanned extensions are `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.js`, `.mjs`, `.cjs`, `.mts`, `.cts`, and test and spec files are included. The policy is zero-config and strict, with no allowlist.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Lists every tracked file with each extension, combining and de-duplicating results; no directory is skipped.\n- Matches `eslint-disable(-next-line|-line)?` — file-level `eslint-disable`, block `eslint-disable ... eslint-enable`, `eslint-disable-line`, and `eslint-disable-next-line`.\n- Pass: no scanned file contains a directive.\n- Fail: `path/to/file.ts:42: <directive>` per hit.\n- `filesChecked` counts every scanned source file (after de-duplication).\n",
	"no-fitness-front-matter":      "Fails a Markdown file whose front matter still holds `fitnessFunctions` or `relatedConfigurations`. No check reads either key, so a leftover block only teaches agents to copy it.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: no tracked .md file's leading front matter holds either key.\n- Fail: one error per file holding one, naming the file and telling you to run `fitness strip-front-matter`.\n- `filesChecked` counts the .md files judged, test fixtures left out.\n\nIt reads a leading `---` block, or the same block inside a leading HTML comment, as a PR template carries it. A byte order mark or CRLF line endings change nothing. Other keys, such as an issue template's `name` and `labels`, pass. A leading `---` before prose is a rule, so it passes too.\n\nTest fixtures are out of scope. Files under a `testdata` or `tests/examples` folder are never judged, so fixture repos may keep their blocks as test data.\n",
	"no-plans-dir":                 "Guards the plans-to-Issues migration: it fails when any file exists under `docs/plans/`, because plans now live as `Plan`-labeled GitHub Issues, not checked-in files. It points the author to open a Plan Issue instead.\n\nIt is wired into the file suite, so the pre-commit hook catches a `docs/plans/` file before it lands.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: no file exists under `docs/plans/`.\n- Fail: one error per file found there, naming the file and the fix.\n- `filesChecked` is the number of offending files (zero when clean).\n",
	"node-version":                 "Requires the machine's active Node version to satisfy the repo's `.nvmrc`. It only compares; run `nvm use` yourself if needed. It asks `node --version` for the current version, and a missing node binary is a clear failure.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: `.nvmrc` exists and its major version is at most the current Node's major, as `.nvmrc` holding `24` and Node v24.x or higher.\n- Fail: `.nvmrc` missing → `missing .nvmrc`.\n- Fail: Node too old → `Node vX.Y.Z does not satisfy .nvmrc (requires N.x). Run: nvm use`.\n\nIt accepts plain or `v`-prefixed versions in `.nvmrc`, such as `24` or `v24`.\n",
	"plan-structure":               "Validates that a plan, a GitHub Issue body under the `Plan` label, follows the [Plan issue template](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/plan.md). It requires a one-line blockquote pitch, a `## Background` section, and a `## What needs to happen` section with at least one checklist item. No other `##` sections are allowed.\n\nA repo's own `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE` file labeled `Plan` replaces the sections below. A section whose template shows a checklist item needs a checklist item.\n\nPlans no longer live as repo files, so this check reads its target from stdin (or a `--body-file` path, `-` meaning stdin). It falls back to the runner's context-inline `--body` value. With no input at all it passes with zero files checked, so the file runner never trips on it. It is driven by the [issue-check workflow](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/.github/workflows/README.md), not the local suite.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: a non-placeholder blockquote pitch before the first `##`, and a `## Background` section.\n- Pass: a `## What needs to happen` section with a `- [ ]` or `- [x]` item, and no other `##` sections.\n- Fail: a missing pitch, a placeholder pitch, a missing section, an empty checklist, or any extra `##` section — each its own error.\n- Fail (pointed): an `## Open questions` section reports a message to turn unknowns into checklist steps instead.\n\nFenced code is skipped, so a `##` or checkbox inside a code sample never counts.\n",
	"plan-trailer":                 "Validates an optional `Plan #NN` commit trailer. A commit may name the Plan Issue it implements with a line reading exactly `Plan #<number>`. The trailer is optional, so a message without one passes. A plan reference that is present must be well formed.\n\nA Plan in another repo of the same owner, such as an org-wide Plan in the org's `.github` repo, reads `Plan <owner>/<repo>#<number>` or `Plan [https://github.com/](https://github.com/)<owner>/<repo>/issues/<number>`. The trailer goes on the branch's first commit only, since [issue-link-once](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-issue-link-once/README.md) fails a repeat in any of the spellings.\n\nLike `semantic-commit`, it reads the proposed message from the context-inline `--message` value, falling back to the HEAD commit message. The `commit-msg` hook runs it beside `semantic-commit`.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: the message has no plan-reference line, or its reference reads exactly one of the three spellings.\n- Fail: a plan-reference line in another spelling, one error each, with a fix hint naming all three.\n- `Plan 63`, `plan #63`, `Plan: 63` and `Plan may-journal/.github 63` all fail.\n- Body prose is never a trailer. A line like \"Plan the rollout in 3 steps\" is ignored.\n- `filesChecked` is always 1 (the one commit message).\n\nThe pre-push gate accepts a qualified Plan only from the pushing repo's own owner, and reads it from the repo it names.\n",
	"pr-closes-issue":              "Validates that a pull request description closes at least one issue on merge ([#69](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/69)). GitHub auto-closes a linked issue only for a fixed set of keywords. A PR that merely references an issue leaves it open, and this check fails that.\n\nIt runs in [pr-check.yml](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/.github/workflows/pr-check.yml) beside `pr-structure`. It reads the PR body from stdin, a `--body-file` path (`-` meaning stdin), or the context-inline `--body`, and passes inert with no input. For a closed Plan, `fitness pr-check` reads that Plan's own closing targets via `--emit-closed`, and requires the PR to close them too. That flag prints the body's closed issue numbers as a JSON array.\n\n## Behavior\n\nThree rules run over the PR description:\n\n- Every PR carries a closing keyword, such as `close`, `fix`, or `resolve`, followed by `#NN`. A body with only references fails.\n- Every issue the PR says it implements (`Implements #NN` or `Plan #NN`) must be among the closed issues; an unclosed one fails, named individually.\n- When the PR closes a `Plan`, every issue that Plan closes must be closed by the PR too, via `--require-close`.\n\nThe full closing-keyword set is `close`, `closes`, `closed`, `fix`, `fixes`, `fixed`, `resolve`, `resolves`, `resolved`. References like `addresses #12`, `part of #12`, or a bare `#12` never count as a closure. Nor does a keyword quoted in inline or fenced code.\n\nThere is no chore or docs exemption — every PR is held to the first rule.\n\n## Unchecked items\n\nA PR must not close an issue whose checklist is unfinished ([#141](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/141)). For each issue the PR closes, `fitness pr-check` lists every unchecked `- [ ]` item, skipping fenced code. Each one fails the PR, named with its issue number.\n\nTick each item as it is delivered, or stop closing the issue. After merge, `fitness close-check` reopens any issue that still closed with unchecked items.\n\nThe closing-keyword list is GitHub's fixed set; keep it in sync in `closingKeywords` if GitHub's set ever changes.\n",
	"pr-structure":                 "Validates that a pull request description follows the [PR template](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md). It requires a one-line blockquote summary, a `## Background` section, and a `## Changelog` section with a bullet, and no other `##` sections. The \"what changed\" lives in the diff, so the description carries only the why and the changelog.\n\nA PR description is not a file in the tree, so this check reads its target from stdin. It also accepts a `--body-file` path (`-` meaning stdin) and falls back to the runner's context-inline `--body` value. With no input at all it passes with zero files checked, so the file runner never trips on it. It is driven by the [pr-check workflow](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/.github/workflows/README.md), not the local suite.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: a non-placeholder blockquote summary before the first `##`; a `## Background` section; a `## Changelog` with a `-` bullet; no other `##` sections.\n- Fail: a missing or placeholder summary, a missing section, a changelog without bullets, or any extra `##` section — each reported separately.\n\nFenced code is skipped, so a `##` or bullet inside a code sample never counts.\n\nThe bullet links the PR's [entry file](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-changelog/README.md#entry-files) rather than copying it. This check sees only the body, so `pr-check` enforces the link with the PR's diff. It fails a PR that adds an entry without linking it, links another file, or copies a bullet the PR adds.\n",
	"prettier":                     "Runs Prettier `--check` over the repo's files, to ensure they are formatted. It uses a local Prettier config when present, such as `.prettierrc.json`, `prettier.config.cjs`, or a `package.json` `\"prettier\"` field. Otherwise it falls back to the shared `prettier.config.cjs`. That comes from an installed `@mayjournal/fitness-shared` package when present, else the copy embedded in the check binary, materialized on demand.\n\n[Prettier](https://prettier.io) itself is a peer tool: the check execs the real binary, never npx. It resolves from `node_modules/.bin` (walking up from the repo root) then PATH. It fails with a one-line install hint when the binary is missing.\n\nRun `fitness prettier` from your app root to check formatting. Pass through args to Prettier: `fitness prettier --write .` or `fitness prettier --write src/`. The runner forwards args after the check name to the check, which runs at the repo root.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: all checked files use Prettier code style.\n- In a scoped run: only changed paths are checked, passing with 0 files when Prettier parses none.\n- Otherwise: runs `prettier --check` on every tracked file from the repo root.\n- Config: the local project config if present; otherwise the shared prettier config resolved as above, with no copy required in the consumer repo.\n- Plugins: the shared config loads `prettier-plugin-packagejson` and `prettier-plugin-sort-json`, so the repo installs both.\n- Synced files: the shared config keeps `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/**/*.md` and `.github/fitness-setup.md` at Prettier defaults with an [override](https://prettier.io/docs/configuration#configuration-overrides), as they arrive from may-journal/.github.\n- The check puts the repo's `node_modules` folders on `NODE_PATH`, where the shared config finds them.\n- When Prettier fails without naming a file, its own `[error]` lines follow the fallback message, such as a plugin it cannot load.\n\nErrors list file paths that need formatting.\n\n## Biome\n\nA repo that chose Biome formats with it instead, as [ADR 0006](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/fitness/adr-0006/README.md) records. Such a repo has a `biome.json` or `biome.jsonc` at its root and `@biomejs/biome` in its `package.json` dependencies. The check runs `biome format --reporter=github` on the same paths, and needs no Prettier.\n\nBiome resolves like Prettier, and a missing binary fails with an install hint. Biome before 1.8 fails naming its version, since its CLI rejects the GitHub reporter. Each file Biome would reformat fails by name.\n\nPassthrough args are Prettier's, so the check translates them. Paths such as `.` replace the checked paths, and `--write` writes in place. `--check`, `--list-different`, and `--ignore-unknown` already hold, so they drop. Any other flag fails by name, since Biome would read it as a path.\n\n## Every tracked file\n\n- `--ignore-unknown` lets a file type Prettier cannot parse pass, instead of skipping it by name.\n- `--ignore-path /dev/null` and `--with-node-modules` override `.gitignore` and the `node_modules` default.\n- A `.prettierignore` at the root fails, even in a repo without `package.json`; delete it and format the files instead.\n",
	"prose-budget":                 "Caps how much prose a markdown file holds. It is the volume sibling of [text-readability](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-text-readability/README.md), which scores density. Where that check asks whether prose is dense, this one asks whether there is too much of it.\n\nEach `.md` file is masked the same way `text-readability` masks it. Front matter, fenced code, tables, headings, and dash-only lines such as a Measurement's `-` are dropped. Only flowing paragraphs, blockquotes, and list items remain. The check then enforces six hard limits on what is left.\n\n## Limits\n\n- Words per sentence: 23.\n- Sentences per paragraph: 4.\n- Paragraphs per section: 3.\n- Words per list item: 23.\n- Items per list: 8.\n- Prose words per section: 300.\n\nEach limit is overridable in `.fitnessrc.json` under `proseBudget`, as the configuration below lists. A positive value replaces the default, and zero or absent keeps it.\n\nA paragraph is a run of consecutive prose lines. A section runs from one heading to the next, and its paragraphs and lists count toward `maxWords`. Prose before the first heading is its own section. A list is a run of consecutive items; nested items count individually.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: every masked paragraph, section, and list in the file is within all six limits.\n- Fail: one error per limit crossed, naming the file, the offending element, and its count versus the limit.\n- Every tracked `.md` file is judged, `CHANGELOG.md` included; there is no exempt list.\n- A `.fitnessrc.json` that sets `proseBudget.exempt` fails the run.\n\nIt shares its prose extraction and sentence splitting with `text-readability` through `internal/mdx`.\n\n## Configuration\n\nThese `.fitnessrc.json` keys tune the check.\n\n`proseBudget` holds the prose-budget check's hard caps. Each limit falls back to a built-in default when zero.\n\n- `maxSentenceWords` is the most words one sentence may hold.\n- `maxParagraphSentences` is the most sentences one paragraph may hold.\n- `maxSectionParagraphs` is the most paragraphs one section may hold.\n- `maxListItemWords` is the most words one list item may hold.\n- `maxListItems` is the most items one list may hold, and the most bullets changelog-bullets allows in a release section.\n- `maxWords` is the most prose words one section may hold.\n",
	"read-repo-first":              "Reminds agents and users to read the repo's decisions and enabled checks first.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Always passes with zero files checked; no TTY or interactive prompt.\n- Outputs a boxed message to stderr; stdout carries the JSON result.\n- The box shows the question, a table of enabled check names when the runner provides them, and a note about not using `--no-verify`.\n",
	"release-changelog":            "Binds Release Please's manifest to the version file a reviewer can inspect. The check applies when `.release-please-manifest.json` exists and contains a root release version.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Use an explicit availability gate and pass with zero files when the repository has no Release Please manifest.\n- Pass during bootstrap when the manifest is an empty JSON object.\n- Require the root manifest value and `version.txt` to contain the same numeric `major.minor.patch` version.\n- Reject malformed JSON, unsupported manifests without the root `.` package, a missing `version.txt`, and mismatched versions.\n\nRelease notes come from [entry files](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-changelog/README.md#entry-files), not `CHANGELOG.md`, so this check asks nothing of `CHANGELOG.md`. Set `\"skip-changelog\": true` in `release-please-config.json` so Release Please stops writing it.\n",
	"repeated-string-literals":     "Fails a string literal that appears three or more times across the repo's JS and TS sources. It should be a shared constant. When the repeats are a small closed set like `'active'` and `'pending'`, a union type or enum fits better. It is on by default.\n\n## Why not just ESLint or jscpd\n\n- `jscpd` finds duplicated multi-line blocks (`--min-lines 5`); a single repeated literal is one token on one line, so it never sees it.\n- `sonarjs/no-duplicate-string` (ESLint) only flags strings of 10+ chars containing a separator, which excludes the short, identifier-like tokens (`'active'`, `'GET'`) this check targets.\n\n## What passes and fails\n\nA value repeated only twice passes; a value used 3+ times anywhere across the scanned files fails, one error per value, most-repeated first:\n\n```\n// src/a.ts\nif (order.status === 'active') setStatus('active');\n// src/b.ts\nconst rows = all.filter((r) => r.status === 'active');\n```\n\nIt fails with:\n\n```\n\"active\" appears 3 times (src/a.ts:1, src/a.ts:1, src/b.ts:2) — extract a shared constant\n```\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Scans every tracked `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.js`, `.mjs`, `.cjs`, `.mts`, `.cts` file, test, spec, and bench files included.\n- A hand lexer extracts single- and double-quoted literals, ignoring comments, regex, and template literals, and dropping module specifiers (after `import`, `require`, `from`).\n- Counts identical values repo-wide; flags any at or above the threshold (3). Values under the length floor (3) are ignored.\n- Idiomatic tokens are never flagged: buffer encodings (`'utf8'`), stdio modes (`'inherit'`), `typeof` results (`'object'`), and directives (`'use strict'`).\n- Error format: `\"value\" appears N times (file:line, …, +K more) — extract a shared constant`, locations capped at 5.\n- `filesChecked` counts every scanned source file.\n\n## Allow list\n\nUse `allow` for the rare repeat no constant can fix. That means a value that must live once per runtime island — TS sources, published bin scripts, or automation scripts. Or one spelling shared by genuinely different things:\n\n```\n{\n  \"repeatedStringLiterals\": { \"allow\": [\"my-check-name\", \"kindValue\"] }\n}\n```\n\n`allow` entries match exact values and are never flagged. Keep the list a shrinking baseline; the fix is to centralize the constant. A `.fitnessrc.json` that fails to load leaves the allow list empty.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- Template literals are out of scope — only plain `'…'` and `\"…\"` literals count.\n- Thresholds and the idiomatic-token set are fixed in the binary; there is no inline marker and no enum-candidate detection yet (tracked in #44).\n\n## Configuration\n\nThese `.fitnessrc.json` keys tune the check.\n\n`repeatedStringLiterals` holds options for that check.\n\n- `allow` lists exact values the check never flags, for the rare repeat no shared constant can fix. Keep it a shrinking list.\n",
	"requirements":                 "Enforces docs/requirements in every repo a language module covers. In a repo written in any language of the [module registry](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/langs/langs.go), each file in `docs/requirements` is one requirement.\n\nA requirement says why the requirement matters, how to measure it, and which acceptances prove it. Each acceptance is owned by exactly one test named for it, and each test owns exactly one acceptance. A test that proves no requirement is either a requirement nobody wrote down or a duplicate to delete.\n\n## Template\n\nCopy this into `docs/requirements/NNNN-kebab-title.md`. The comments explain each rule.\n\nWrite it as Biome formats Markdown: blank lines around the `-`, so it never underlines the numerator as a heading, and two-space nesting.\n\n```\n<!-- File name is NNNN-kebab-title.md. NNNN matches the title ID, is unique in docs/requirements, and is never reused after deletion. -->\n# 0001 Offline Sync\n\n## Why\n<!-- One sentence: what a person can do once this holds. -->\n\nI can keep writing when signal drops or is lost\n\n## Measurement\n<!-- Numerator line, a \"-\" line, denominator line, each set apart by blank lines. Nothing else: no \"Source:\" line, since a code line number goes stale when the code moves. -->\n\nusers who save locally\n\n-\n\nusers who have lost connection\n\n## Requirements\n<!-- Top-level bullets are IDs: title ID + \".N\", unique, never reused. -->\n<!-- Each ID nests exactly one Given, one When, one Then, one level (two spaces) deeper each, each line starting with its keyword. A second Given, When, or Then is a new ID. -->\n<!-- A Given starts with an actor the system context declares, by name or Mermaid ID; see the architecture-actors check. -->\n<!-- Exactly one test is named for each ID, the way its language module names tests; the check's error names the test it expects. Delete the test when you delete the ID. -->\n- 0001.1\n  - Given Writer has no network\n    - When I edit an entry\n      - Then it is saved locally\n```\n\n## Rules\n\n- IDs: the file name's `NNNN` matches the title, and no two docs share it or one area, per [ADR 0004](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/fitness/adr-0004/README.md).\n- Acceptance IDs: the title ID plus `.N`, unique in its doc.\n- Reuse: git history records every ID a commit removed. A doc or acceptance that brings one back fails.\n- Sections: exactly `## Why`, `## Measurement`, `## Requirements`, in that order, none empty.\n- Measurement: numerator, `-`, denominator, and no `Source:` line. A leftover one gets a note; `fitness requirements --fix` deletes them.\n- Acceptance: one Given, one When, and one Then, nested in order; the Given starts with an [actor](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-architecture-actors/README.md).\n- Ownership: exactly one test named for each acceptance owns it. A test named for an undefined acceptance fails.\n- Every test: each top-level test in the repository must be named for an acceptance. Setup functions and helpers are not tests.\n\nEach language module finds its tests and names the test that owns an ID, as its package documents. A comment or string never counts as a test.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- No code: a repo with no file in a module's language passes with `filesChecked: 0`.\n- Not code: a file a module names a tool config, or a file `.gitattributes` marks `linguist-generated`.\n- Missing docs: a repo with such code and none in `docs/requirements` fails.\n- Full and scoped runs judge every doc and test file, including unchanged files in pull requests that change only unrelated files.\n- Ownership is a repository-wide invariant: an unmapped repo stays red until every test owns a requirement.\n\n## Swift tests\n\nSwift requirement tests run on Swift Testing with [apple-ui-testing-library](https://github.com/may-journal/apple-ui-testing-library), hosted in the app, natively as Designed for iPad or on macOS. The [Swift module](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/swift/library.go) judges them.\n\n- Imports: a test file fails on XCTest or another UI test framework, such as ViewInspector or SnapshotTesting.\n- Calls: a test file fails on an XCTestCase subclass, Xcode UI tests such as XCUIApplication, or simctl.\n- Library: a test file fails unless it, or a file in its folder or below, imports `UITestingLibrary`.\n- End-to-end: a file that imports `UITestingLibrarySystem` may use XCTest and simctl, and drives the app through its `SystemSession`.\n\n## Pairs\n\nA requirement test and its doc change together, so neither drifts from the other. Changed behavior extends its existing doc, as [ADR 0004](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/fitness/adr-0004/README.md) sets out. A doc whose words stay the same line by line, as after `biome format`, has not changed. Nor has one whose only change is to its leftover Source line.\n\n- Pairing: a test file pairs with each doc whose number its name or its tests carry, one or several.\n- Tests: a changed test file fails unless one of its docs changed too. The error names both files and the missing doc.\n- Fixes: a changed test file that failed this check at its base passes without its doc, since its doc still holds.\n- Base check: this check judges a checkout of the base commit's docs and test files, where a Swift test importing XCTest fails.\n- New files: a test file the base lacks still needs its doc.\n- Docs: a changed doc fails unless one of its test files changed too. The error names both files and the missing test.\n- Helpers: a test file that names and holds no requirement pairs with nothing.\n- No exemptions: there is no trailer, flag, or body text that lets an unpaired change through.\n\nWhich changes are judged depends on the run:\n\n- Commits: the `pre-commit` hook judges the staged files, and HEAD is their base.\n- Amends: an amended commit is judged on everything it changes since its parent, which is their base.\n- Pull requests: CI judges the diff against its base, the merge base with the base branch, so merged base changes are not judged.\n- Replays: a local merge, cherry-pick, or revert, or an amended merge, is not judged again. Its pull request diff still is.\n- Full scans: a run with nothing staged, or a push to the default branch, judges no pairs.\n\n## Requirement checks\n\nSome requirements are rules over the repo's own files. A check in `fitness/req-NNNN/`, one of the repo's [local checks](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/docs/local-checks.md), may prove such a doc.\n\n- Ownership: such a doc's acceptances need no test. A test named for one still owns it, and two still fail.\n- Orphans: a `fitness/req-NNNN/` folder whose number no doc defines fails.\n- Pairs: the check's files pair with the doc as a test file does, so a change to one half alone fails.\n- Broken scan: a requirement check that passes having judged no files fails the run.\n",
	"requirements-changed":         "Fails a change that adds or changes product code without also changing a test and a requirement doc. The [requirements](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/cmd/fitness-check-requirements/README.md) check judges the docs and tests that exist; this check judges what a commit or pull request leaves out.\n\n## What counts\n\n- Product code: `.go` and `.swift` files that are not tests, and `.sh` scripts.\n- Vendored code is not product code: files under `vendor`, `third_party`, `Pods`, `Carthage`, or `node_modules`.\n- Fixtures and build output are not either: Go `testdata`, `dist`, `.build`, `DerivedData`, and what each [language module](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/langs/langs.go) names.\n- Generated files are not: those `.gitattributes` marks `linguist-generated`, and Go files that say `Code generated … DO NOT EDIT.`\n- Tests: `_test.go` files, and Swift files that declare a test or sit in a `…Tests` folder, as the requirements check pairs them.\n- Requirement docs: `docs/requirements/NNNN-*.md`.\n\nA file counts as changed only when its lines change beyond whitespace, so a pure rename or a reformat changes nothing.\n\n## Passes\n\n- Product code changes beside a changed test and a changed requirement doc.\n- No product code changed, as in a docs-only, config-only, or `CHANGELOG.md`-only change.\n- A pure rename or a whitespace-only edit.\n- A local merge, cherry-pick, or revert, or an amended merge, is not judged again. Its pull request diff still is.\n- A repo with no `docs/requirements/NNNN-*.md` doc yet.\n- A full scan, or a `--dir` folder, judges nothing.\n- A deletion of product code that no requirement doc names.\n\n## Fails\n\n- Product code changed without a test, a requirement doc, or both. The error names the file and the missing half.\n- Product code deleted while a requirement doc still names it. The error names the file, the doc, and the doc's tests.\n\nA deletion passes when that doc or one of its tests changes with it.\n\n## No exceptions\n\nNo commit trailer, flag or setting turns the check off for one change.\n\n## When it runs\n\n- Commits: the staged files. With a `commit-msg` hook, the hook judges them.\n- A `commit-msg` hook, as `fitness init` installs, also makes `pre-commit` leave the check out.\n- Amends: everything the amended commit changes since its parent.\n- Pull requests: CI judges the diff against the merge base with the base branch.\n",
	"semantic-commit":              "Requires the commit subject to follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/), `type(scope): description`, with the scope required. A merge commit (`Merge ...`) is always accepted.\n\n## Allowed types\n\nThe types come from [conventional-commit-types](https://github.com/commitizen/conventional-commit-types), inlined in the check binary: the same eleven types, in the same order.\n\nScope is required, as in `feat(api): add endpoint`. Merge commits are always accepted.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: the subject matches `type(scope): description` or `Merge ...`. Only the first line is judged.\n- Fail: the subject doesn’t match → error quoting it, with the suggested format and allowed types.\n- Fail (empty message, or no repo to read): a no-message error.\n\n## Which message it judges\n\n- commit-msg hook: the message file being written, passed as `--message`.\n- `pr-check` workflow: the PR title, passed the same way. A `--message` argument always wins.\n- pre-commit hook with a commit-msg hook beside it: none. The new message does not exist yet, and HEAD is only its parent.\n- pre-commit hook alone, such as `fitness-install --install-hook`: the branch’s earlier commits, as a full run judges them. A bad subject fails the next commit.\n- Full run, such as `fitness --all`: each of the branch’s own commits, never history inherited from its base.\n\nA full run finds the base the way the `local-branch` check does. A pull request run in GitHub Actions starts from the fetched `origin/$GITHUB_BASE_REF`. Locally, a branch counts the commits no other origin branch holds, whatever the default branch is called.\n\nThe default branch is `origin/HEAD`, else a fetched `origin/main` or `origin/master`. On it, on a detached HEAD, or while the default is unknown, only commits origin lacks count. With no commits of its own, the check passes with 0 checked.\n\nA repo with no origin refs inherits nothing, so a full run judges the HEAD commit subject.\n",
	"spotless":                     "Checks Java formatting with the [Spotless](https://github.com/diffplug/spotless) plugin the repo's own build declares. The Java language module owns the build run and its judgment. It is on by default.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Runs once from each build root: `spotless:check` for Maven, `spotlessCheck` for Gradle.\n- The [Java module](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/go/internal/lang/java/java.go) says what a build root is and which wrapper or tool runs it.\n- Pass: Spotless finds nothing to rewrite.\n- Fail: each file Spotless would rewrite, as `file is not formatted; run spotless:apply` (or `spotlessApply`).\n- A failure that names no file reports the build's own error lines.\n- No Maven or Gradle build in the repo: passes clean with `filesChecked: 0`.\n- A Java repo with no running JDK fails with an install hint.\n- A build that does not declare Spotless fails with the plugin snippet to add.\n\n## Formatter\n\nThe build's own Spotless block picks the formatter, so the check never imposes a style. The hint suggests Google Java Format.\n",
	"swiftlint":                    "Runs SwiftLint over the repo through the Swift language module, which owns the tool run and its judgment, via `swiftlint lint --strict`. [SwiftLint](https://github.com/realm/SwiftLint) is a system binary. It is on by default.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Runs: `swiftlint lint --strict --reporter json --quiet .` from repo root.\n- Pass: no violations.\n- Fail: any violation — reported as `file:line[:col] - reason (rule_id)`.\n- No Swift files in the repo: passes clean (`filesChecked: 0`) instead of erroring, so it is safe in every repo.\n- SwiftLint is not an npm package but a system binary (`brew install swiftlint`), resolved from PATH at runtime, never `node_modules`.\n- A missing binary fails clearly with a one-line install hint rather than a stack trace.\n\n## SwiftLint config\n\nIt uses whatever `.swiftlint.yml` is present in the repo root — the same \"consumer config wins\" convention as the other checks. No shared-config fallback ships for this one yet.\n",
	"text-readability":             "Fails a markdown file whose prose scores as hard to read. It is a document-level readability smoke detector. Each `.md` file is scored with three character-based formulas — Coleman-Liau, ARI, and LIX. A file fails only when at least two of the three sit in alarm territory.\n\nThe bands, the ensemble vote, and the masking rules come from this repo's own corpus in [docs/research/0001-prose-cognitive-complexity.md](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/blob/main/docs/research/0001-prose-cognitive-complexity.md). Well-written technical prose here runs grade 11-15 and LIX 40-48. The alarms fire on genuine outliers — a 60-word run-on, notation masquerading as prose — never on ordinary dense prose.\n\nLower scores are not better. Readability formulas reward chatty filler and punish information density. On this repo's plans they rank the preferred style as harder. This check is a ceiling on absurdity, not a target to optimize.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Whole documents only, never paragraphs: files under 100 prose words are counted but not judged, since below that the formulas are statistically meaningless.\n- Frozen masking spec before scoring: front matter and HTML comments dropped; fenced code, headings, tables, and dash-only lines skipped.\n- Inline code spans become a placeholder word; link text kept, URLs and version tokens dropped.\n- Every block end (heading, list item, blank line) counts as a sentence boundary.\n- The formulas use only letter, word, and sentence counts — no syllable guessing, no word lists — so the check is deterministic.\n- Scores are implementation-defined; the thresholds are calibrated against this implementation, not the literature.\n- Alarm requires 2 of 3: Coleman-Liau >= 18, ARI >= 18, LIX >= 60 (defaults).\n- `--report` (passthrough arg) prints every file's scores to stderr without changing the verdict.\n\n## Errors\n\nPer-file alarms come first. A failing run appends three guidance lines: the formulas, the edits that lower them, and where the methodology lives. An LLM or human can fix the prose from the error output alone:\n\n```\ndocs/foo.md: readability alarm on 412 prose words — Coleman-Liau 19.2 and ARI 18.6 (grade bands, alarm at 18), LIX 63.0 (alarm at 60)\nformulas: Coleman-Liau = 5.88(letters/word) - 29.6(sentences/word) - 15.8; ARI = 4.71(letters/word) + 0.5(words/sentence) - 21.43; LIX = words/sentence + 100(share of 7+ letter words); all three rise with long words and long sentences, and a file fails when 2 of 3 exceed their band\nto fix: split sentences over ~25 words (semicolon and em-dash chains count as ONE sentence — end them with periods); wrap identifiers, file names, and check names in backticks (code spans are masked to one short word before scoring, so unbackticked notation reads as very long words); move quoted tool output and name lists into fenced code blocks, which are never scored\nmethodology: go/cmd/fitness-check-text-readability/README.md (behavior, masking spec, bands) and docs/research/0001-prose-cognitive-complexity.md (why these formulas, calibration on this repo, their limits)\n```\n\n## Bands\n\nOptional bands in `.fitnessrc.json`, where any positive value overrides its default:\n\n```\n{\n  \"textReadability\": { \"maxGrade\": 18, \"maxLix\": 60, \"minWords\": 100 }\n}\n```\n\n## Configuration\n\nThese `.fitnessrc.json` keys tune the check.\n\n`textReadability` holds alarm bands for the text-readability check.\n\n- `maxGrade` is the Coleman-Liau and ARI grade at which a file alarms.\n- `maxLix` is the LIX score at which a file alarms.\n- `minWords` is the fewest prose words a file needs to be judged.\n",
	"vitest-coverage-exclude":      "Fails any Vitest coverage exclude entry, so coverage measures every file.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- Pass: no Vitest config, or `coverage.exclude` is empty or missing.\n- Fail: any exclude entry, tests and type files included → one error per entry, naming it to remove.\n- Vitest's own default exclude skips tests and declarations, so set `coverage.exclude: []` to measure them; the shared config does.\n\nIt reads `vitest.config.*` or the `package.json` `vitest` field in the project root. If none exist, it uses the shared vitest config. That is an installed `@mayjournal/fitness-shared` package when present, else the copy embedded in the check binary, materialized on demand.\n\nIt reads the config as text, so exclude entries built from variables, spreads, or imports are invisible to it.\n",
	"vitest-coverage-full":         "Requires Vitest coverage thresholds of 100 in the repo and the shared config, then runs `vitest run --coverage`.\n\n## Behavior\n\nA pass requires all three of:\n\n- The project sets Vitest coverage thresholds to 100 for branches, functions, lines, and statements.\n- The fitness-runner shared config carries the same 100% thresholds.\n- `vitest run --coverage` exits 0 in the project root.\n\nBoth configs are checked statically, as text. Neither side may lower thresholds to pass.\n\n- Threshold and exclude settings come from the project's vitest config, else the shared `vitest.config.mjs` (installed `@mayjournal/fitness-shared` package or the binary's embedded copy).\n- When the project has no local vitest config, `vitest run --coverage` runs with `--config` pointing at that resolved file.\n- The vitest binary resolves from `node_modules/.bin` (walking up from root), then PATH — never npx. A missing binary fails with an install hint.\n\n## Errors\n\n```\nVitest coverage thresholds must be 100 for branches, functions, lines, and statements.\nFitness-runner package must have Vitest coverage thresholds set to 100 for branches, functions, lines, and statements.\n```\n\nA failing coverage run reports the last line of vitest output, or a fallback hint when there is none:\n\n```\nVitest coverage did not meet 100% thresholds. Run: npm run test\n```\n",
	"workflow-ref-exists":          "Fails a workflow that calls a may-journal workflow or action at a tag or sha where that path does not exist yet ([#485](https://github.com/may-journal/fitness-runner/issues/485)). Such a caller fails every event in its repo with a missing-workflow error, as fitness-runner#249 did.\n\n## Behavior\n\n- It reads each `uses:` line in the workflow files directly under `.github/workflows`.\n- A `may-journal/<repo>/<path>@<ref>` line names a reusable workflow file or an action folder. A bare `may-journal/<repo>@<ref>` names the repo's root action.\n- A ref is a tag or a full 40-character sha, and the path must exist at it.\n- An action folder holds an `action.yml` or `action.yaml` file at the ref.\n- Fail: the path is missing at the ref, naming the file, line, path, and ref.\n- Fail: the ref is not a tag, a branch, or a full sha, or a sha names no commit.\n- Ignored: local `./` actions, other owners, branch refs, and refs built from `${{ }}` expressions.\n- Ignored: text inside a `|` or `>` block, such as a `run:` script.\n\nIn a pull request, or a staged commit, it reads only the changed workflow files. Composite actions outside `.github/workflows` are out of scope.\n\n## GitHub access\n\nThe check asks GitHub through `gh api`, so it uses `gh`'s login or `GH_TOKEN`. The org's `fitness-suite` and CI workflows pass `GH_TOKEN` to the suite for it.\n\nWhen GitHub cannot answer, it never passes or fails a ref it could not see. No `gh`, no network, a bad token, a rate limit, or 20 seconds of reads stops it. It keeps the findings so far and notes the calls left unverified. When the runner's probe found GitHub's API host unreachable, it asks nothing and reports info naming the host and proxy.\n\nGitHub refusing one request, such as a 403, skips that call with a note. A repo that answers 404 is one the token cannot read, so its calls are skipped with a note too. A workflow token reads only its own repo and public ones, so private repos are skipped in CI.\n",
}
